QB.add(40,[{"id":40001,"t":"Case 1: Urinary Tract Infection","s":"A 32-year-old woman has 48 hours of dysuria, frequency and urgency, with a temperature of 39 °C. Her urine dip-stick shows protein, blood, leucocytes and nitrites. Which dip-stick finding is the classic marker of bacterial infection?","o":["Nitrites","Proteinuria, which shows protein has leaked into the urine","Haematuria, which shows blood is present in the urine","Leucocytes, which show white cells are present in the urine","A normal pH, which rules out any infection"],"e":"Her dip-stick showed proteinuria, haematuria, leucocytes and nitrites. Nitrites are classic for an infection: they are made by bacteria metabolising nitrogen waste. The dip-stick is done in your own office and does not need to go to the lab.","w":["Protein in the urine is present but is not the classic bacterial marker.","Blood is present but is not specific to bacterial metabolism.","Leucocytes show an immune response but nitrites are the bacterial marker.","A normal pH does not rule out infection and was not the finding described."],"k":"Nitrites on dip-stick = bacterial metabolism of nitrogen waste.","rn":[["","Why do nitrites indicate infection?","Why do nitrites indicate infection?","Bacteria metabolise; Nitrogen waste"],["","Which urine dipstick finding is classic for infection?","Which urine dipstick finding is classic for infection?","Nitrites"]]},{"id":40002,"t":"Case 1: Urinary Tract Infection","s":"In the same patient, which findings argue AGAINST pyelonephritis?","o":["No loin tenderness and not systemically ill","A fever of 39 °C and a pulse of 90 per minute","Suprapubic tenderness and nitrites on dip-stick","Leucocytes and blood in the urine","Burning pain and urinary frequency"],"e":"Pyelonephritis is infection of the upper renal tract (spread further up). There was no evidence of it: no loin tenderness, and she was not systemically ill, which the lecturer stressed is really important. Her symptoms seemed localised, so she was treated with an antibiotic.","w":["Fever and tachycardia were present and do not argue against pyelonephritis.","Suprapubic tenderness and nitrites point to a lower UTI but do not exclude upper spread.","These urine findings occur in UTI and do not exclude pyelonephritis.","These are lower urinary symptoms and do not exclude upper spread."],"k":"No loin tenderness + not systemically ill → no evidence of pyelonephritis.","rn":[["","What 2 findings show no evidence of pyelonephritis?","What 2 findings show no evidence of pyelonephritis?","No loin tenderness; Not systemically ill"]]},{"id":40003,"t":"Case 1: Urinary Tract Infection","s":"The GP prescribes trimethoprim for 5 days. Which statement about this choice is consistent with the lecture?","o":["It is cheap, safe and narrow-spectrum, with about a 30% chance of resistance","It is broad-spectrum and is guaranteed to work against every strain of E. coli in the community","It was chosen because she had previous treatment failures","It is the most expensive option and so the most effective","It is given only after lab results are back"],"e":"With no history of previous treatment or of an abnormal renal tract, trimethoprim was chosen: cheap, safe and narrow-spectrum, with probably a 30% chance her infection is resistant, a problem that will grow. Treatment is empiric, started before lab results, and the GP still sends a sample to microbiology.","w":["Trimethoprim is narrow-spectrum, and resistance is possible.","She had no history of previous treatment.","It was chosen partly because it is cheap.","Treatment starts empirically, before results."],"k":"Trimethoprim: cheap, safe, narrow-spectrum; ~30% resistance risk.","rn":[["","What are the 3 advantages of trimethoprim?","What are the 3 advantages of trimethoprim?","Cheap; Safe; Narrow spectrum"]]},{"id":40004,"t":"Case 1: Urinary Tract Infection","s":"Why might a GP choose a narrow-spectrum drug first rather than a broad-spectrum one, according to the class discussion?","o":["It is often cheaper, and with about a 70% chance of response it can make sense","Narrow-spectrum drugs always work better than broad-spectrum drugs against urinary bacteria","Broad-spectrum drugs cannot be given to women","Lab results are always available within an hour","Narrow-spectrum drugs never meet resistance"],"e":"The narrow-spectrum drug is often cheaper, so it can come down to economics; with about a 70% chance of response it can make sense. The lecturer warned that this window is getting smaller as we face an antibiotic crisis, and many GPs go straight to amoxicillin, especially if symptoms are severe: it is clinical judgement.","w":["They do not always work better; there is a ~30% resistance risk here.","No such restriction is described.","Results take 2–3 days.","Resistance to narrow-spectrum drugs occurs and is rising."],"k":"Narrow-spectrum first: cost and ~70% response, but resistance is rising.","rn":[["","Why might a GP (general practitioner) start with narrow-spectrum trimethoprim rather th…","Why might a GP (general practitioner) start with narrow-spectrum trimethoprim rather than a broad-spectrum antibiotic?","Narrow-spectrum drug is cheaper; Probably ~70% chance she responds"]]},{"id":40005,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"On day 3 the lab reports E. coli, trimethoprim resistant, amoxicillin sensitive. What should the GP do?","o":["Call the patient back and prescribe amoxicillin","Continue trimethoprim because it was the first choice","Stop all antibiotics because E. coli is a commensal","Repeat the urine dip-stick before any change","Wait another week for a second culture"],"e":"The day 3 result showed E. coli, trimethoprim resistant and amoxicillin sensitive, so the GP called the patient back and prescribed amoxicillin. Diagnostic microbiology corrects the initial diagnosis, focuses treatment by identifying the bacterium and its resistance, and informs epidemiology.","w":["The organism is resistant to trimethoprim, so continuing it would not help.","She has a symptomatic infection needing treatment.","The dip-stick does not give sensitivities; the culture already has.","Waiting would delay effective treatment."],"k":"Switch to the drug the lab shows the organism is sensitive to."},{"id":40006,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Why does conventional diagnosis of a UTI usually take 2–3 days?","o":["About 24 h to grow and up to 48 h for antibiotic resistance, plus lab delays","The dip-stick must be incubated in the laboratory for 3 days before it can be read","Urine microscopy takes 48 hours to complete","Gram staining takes several days","Agar must be prepared from seaweed for each sample"],"e":"The sample may not be processed until later in the day unless marked urgent. It takes about 24 hours to grow and give first information, and up to 48 hours for antibiotic resistance (definitive information). Realistically the answer takes 2–3 days, so you must manage the patient in the meantime.","w":["The dip-stick is read immediately in the office.","Urine microscopy takes about 10 seconds.","A Gram stain takes minutes.","Plates are prepared in advance, not per sample over days."],"k":"Culture: 24 h any info, 48 h definitive; ~2–3 days overall.","rn":[["","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","Tracks antibiotic resistance; Spots public health threats; (Notifiable diseases)"]]},{"id":40007,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a role of diagnostic microbiology?","o":["Replacing the need for clinical judgement","Correcting the initial diagnosis","Focusing treatment by identifying the organism and its resistance","Informing epidemiology","Tracking how many bacteria are becoming resistant"],"e":"The case illustrates that diagnostic microbiology corrects your initial diagnosis, focuses your treatment and informs the epidemiology, for example tracking resistance and spotting public health problems. Interpreting the lab data remains the clinician's job, so it does not replace clinical judgement.","w":["Correcting the diagnosis is a listed role.","Focusing treatment is a listed role.","Informing epidemiology is a listed role.","Tracking resistance is part of informing epidemiology."],"k":"Lab: corrects diagnosis, focuses treatment, informs epidemiology."},{"id":40008,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","a":["empiric","empirical","empiric treatment","empirical treatment","empiric therapy"],"s":"What term describes treatment given before the causative organism is known, based on knowledge of likely bacteria and resistance rates?","e":"Treating infection is \"empiric\": it requires knowledge of which bacteria cause which syndromes and of resistance rates, because lab results take 2–3 days.","k":"Empiric treatment starts before the organism is known.","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of knowledge does empiric treatment need?","What 2 kinds of knowledge does empiric treatment need?","Which bacteria cause which syndromes; Resistance rates"],["","What is empiric treatment?","What is empiric treatment?","Treating before lab results"]]},{"id":40009,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Why does \"informing the epidemiology\" matter, according to the lecture?","o":["It tracks resistance and can spot public health problems, including notifiable diseases","It decides which agar plates and discs each hospital laboratory must buy each year","It replaces the need to treat individual patients","It only matters for viral infections","It shows which patients are allergic to penicillin"],"e":"Informing the epidemiology tracks how many bacteria are becoming antibiotic resistant and spots possible public health problems: an organism need not be highly virulent, being highly infectious is enough. Many diseases are notifiable and must be reported to Public Health England (or the equivalent).","w":["Agar purchasing is not the purpose described.","Patients still need treatment.","It applies to bacterial resistance too.","Allergy is not part of this role."],"k":"Epidemiology: resistance tracking, public health, notifiable diseases.","rn":[["","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","Tracks antibiotic resistance; Spots public health threats; (Notifiable diseases)"]]},{"id":40010,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"What white blood cell count in urine is considered \"significant\"?","o":["More than 1×10<super>5</super> per ml","More than 10 per ml","More than 1×10<super>2</super> per ml","Any number above zero is always significant","More than 1×10<super>9</super> per ml"],"e":"Urine microscopy takes about 10 seconds: count the white blood cells, which should be absent in normal urine. More than 1×10<super>5</super>/ml (100,000/ml) is \"significant\". You may also see bacteria, and immune cells plus bacteria make infection easy to call.","w":["This threshold is far too low.","This threshold is too low.","WBCs should be absent, but the significance threshold given is 10<super>5</super>/ml.","This threshold is far too high."],"k":"Significant urine WBCs: >1×10<super>5</super>/ml.","rn":[["","What white cell count in urine is \"significant\"?","What white cell count in urine is \"significant\"?",">1x10^5 / ml (100,000)"]]},{"id":40011,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"In this urine microscopy image, what does label 2 show?","o":["Bacteria (small rods)","White blood cells, which should not be in urine","Red blood cells from haematuria","Crystals of uric acid","Pinworm ova"],"e":"The urine microscopy figure labels 1 white blood cells (round cells that should not be in urine) and 2 bacteria (small rods, arrowed). Immune cells plus bacteria make it easy to call an infection.","w":["White blood cells are label 1.","Red cells are not labelled in this figure.","Crystals are not labelled.","Pinworm ova are seen on the sellotape test, not urine."],"k":"Urine microscopy: WBCs + bacteria = infection.","f":"lm11"},{"id":40012,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A sputum sample may contain a respiratory pathogen that needs blood factors (X, haem) to grow. Which agar is most suitable?","o":["Chocolate agar","CLED agar, which is used for bacteria isolated from urine","Plain agar mixed only with water and salts","Agar containing antibiotics to kill all respiratory bacteria","A sellotape strip for direct visualisation"],"e":"Chocolate agar is a variant of blood agar in which the red cells are lysed by slowly heating to 80 °C. It grows some fastidious respiratory pathogens that need blood factors (X, haem), and it contains no chocolate. Blood agar grows fastidious organisms and shows haemolysis; CLED is for urine.","w":["CLED is for urine samples.","Plain agar lacks the blood factors needed.","Killing all bacteria would prevent culture.","The sellotape test is for pinworm."],"k":"Chocolate agar: lysed red cells for fastidious respiratory pathogens.","rn":[["","What is chocolate agar used to grow?","What is chocolate agar used to grow?","Fastidious respiratory pathogens; Bacteria needing blood factors; (X, haem)"],["","How is chocolate agar made?","How is chocolate agar made?","Blood agar, red cells lysed; Slow heating to 80°C"]]},{"id":40013,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Which pairing of agar and use is correct?","o":["CLED: culturing bacteria from urine","Blood agar: identifying lactose fermenters in urine","Chocolate agar: contains chocolate to feed bacteria","CLED: growing respiratory pathogens needing haem","Blood agar: red cells lysed by heating to 80 °C"],"e":"Blood agar grows fastidious bacteria and shows haemolytic activity. Chocolate agar has red cells lysed by slowly heating to 80 °C and grows fastidious respiratory pathogens needing blood factors (X, haem); it contains no chocolate. CLED (Cystine Lactose Electrolyte Deficient) agar is for culturing bacteria from urine.","w":["Lactose fermentation is read on CLED.","Chocolate agar contains no chocolate.","Respiratory pathogens needing haem grow on chocolate agar.","Heating to lyse red cells makes chocolate agar."],"k":"Blood: fastidious + haemolysis; chocolate: lysed RBC; CLED: urine.","rn":[["","What is CLED agar used for?","What is CLED agar used for?","Bacteria isolated from urine"]]},{"id":40014,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","a":["agar"],"s":"What is the name of the seaweed-derived polysaccharide used to culture bacteria on plates?","e":"Agar is a polysaccharide derived from seaweed. It is mixed with water and additives such as blood, potato starch, sugars, salts, antibiotics and pH indicators to culture bacteria.","k":"Agar: seaweed polysaccharide for culture.","rn":[["","What is agar?","What is agar?","Polysaccharide derived from seaweed"]]},{"id":40015,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Why do some agar plates contain a pH indicator?","o":["To tell lactose users from non-users","To kill fastidious organisms","To lyse red blood cells","To stain bacteria Gram positive","To keep the plate sterile"],"e":"Agar can be mixed with blood, potato starch, sugars, salts, antibiotics and pH indicators. pH indicators help differentiate bacteria that can use lactose (making acid) from those that cannot, as on CLED agar.","w":["pH indicators do not kill organisms.","Red cells are lysed by heat in chocolate agar.","Gram staining is done on slides.","Indicators do not sterilise plates."],"k":"pH indicators reveal lactose fermentation.","rn":[["","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Fermenters: yellow; Non-fermenters: blue"]]},{"id":40016,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"In this image of three agar types, which plate is brown because its red cells have been lysed?","o":["2, chocolate agar","1, blood agar, which is bright red","3, CLED agar, which is blue-green","None of the plates","All three plates"],"e":"The figure shows 1 blood agar (bright red), 2 chocolate agar (brown, lysed red cells) and 3 CLED agar (blue-green before growth).","w":["Blood agar is bright red with intact cells.","CLED is blue-green before growth.","Plate 2 is brown.","Only plate 2 has lysed red cells."],"k":"Chocolate agar is brown from lysed red cells.","f":"lm12","rn":[["","How is chocolate agar made?","How is chocolate agar made?","Blood agar, red cells lysed; Slow heating to 80°C"]]},{"id":40017,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A urine sample is cultured on CLED agar and the colonies and plate turn yellow at 24 hours. What does this indicate?","o":["A lactose fermenter, typical of E. coli","A non-lactose fermenter producing blue colonies","A haemolytic organism on blood agar","A fastidious respiratory pathogen","An acid-fast mycobacterium"],"e":"A bacterium that ferments lactose makes acid (lactic acid); the pH indicator in CLED changes colour, so lactose fermenters give yellow colonies and non-lactose fermenters blue colonies. Yellow on CLED is typical of E. coli: here probably a basic, uncomplicated E. coli infection.","w":["Non-lactose fermenters give blue colonies.","Haemolysis is read on blood agar.","Fastidious respiratory pathogens grow on chocolate agar.","Acid-fast organisms are identified by ZN or auramine stain."],"k":"Yellow CLED = lactose fermenter, e.g. E. coli.","f":"lm13","rn":[["","Image card: Urine culture at 24 hours: what does the yellow colour show? Which organism is typical?","Urine culture at 24 hours: what does the yellow colour show? Which organism is typical?","Lactose fermentation (acid); E. coli"],["","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Fermenters: yellow; Non-fermenters: blue"]]},{"id":40018,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","a":["cled","cled agar","cystine lactose electrolyte deficient agar","cystine lactose electrolyte deficient"],"s":"Name the agar used for culturing bacteria from urine, on which lactose fermenters turn yellow.","e":"CLED (Cystine Lactose Electrolyte (salt) Deficient) agar is used for urine samples. Lactose fermenters make acid and turn yellow; non-lactose fermenters are blue.","k":"CLED agar is for urine cultures.","rn":[["","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Fermenters: yellow; Non-fermenters: blue"],["","What does CLED stand for?","What does CLED stand for?","Cystine Lactose Electrolyte Deficient"],["","What is CLED agar used for?","What is CLED agar used for?","Bacteria isolated from urine"]]},{"id":40019,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"On a disc diffusion plate, one antibiotic disc has a large clear zone around it and another has none. How should these be interpreted?","o":["Clear zone = sensitive; no zone = resistant","Clear zone = resistant; no zone = sensitive","Both indicate the bacteria are dead","Zones show only whether the organism is Gram positive","Zones measure the number of white cells"],"e":"For disc testing, a high density of bacteria is spread across the plate, and filter-paper discs each soaked in one antibiotic are added. The antibiotic diffuses into the agar, and a clear zone of inhibition appears where it stops growth. The zone diameter is compared with disk-diffusion standards: a zone means sensitive, no zone means resistant.","w":["This reverses the interpretation.","Growth around the disc shows living bacteria.","Zones measure antibiotic effect, not Gram status.","Zones do not count white cells."],"k":"Zone of inhibition = sensitive; no zone = resistant.","rn":[["","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone: sensitive; Growth up to disc: resistant"]]},{"id":40020,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which step is part of setting up disc susceptibility testing?","o":["Spreading a high density of bacteria across the plate, not single colonies","Streaking for single colonies only","Heating the plate slowly to 80 °C to lyse the bacteria before adding any discs","Staining the plate with carbolfuchsin","Adding sheep serum to look for a clot"],"e":"For disc testing you spread a high density of bacteria across the plate (not single colonies), add discs soaked in different antibiotics, let the antibiotics diffuse, and measure the clear zone of inhibition against disk-diffusion standards.","w":["Single colonies are not used for this test.","Heating to 80 °C makes chocolate agar.","Carbolfuchsin is the ZN primary stain.","Sheep serum is used in a coagulase test."],"k":"Disc test: lawn of bacteria + antibiotic discs → measure zones.","rn":[["","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","Sample from sterile site; Single pathogen expected"],["","In what forms can bacteria exist?","In what forms can bacteria exist?","Single cells; Pairs; Chains; Clusters"]]},{"id":40021,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"In this susceptibility image, what does label 3 show?","o":["No zone, meaning resistance to that antibiotic","Large clear zones around all four discs on the S. aureus plate","A clear zone, meaning sensitivity to that antibiotic","The E. coli plate as a whole","A colony of lactose fermenters"],"e":"The figure shows 1 Staphylococcus aureus (Gram +) with large clear zones around all four discs (sensitive to all); 2 Escherichia coli (Gram −); 3 no zone, so resistant to this antibiotic; 4 a clear zone, so sensitive to this antibiotic.","w":["The S. aureus plate is label 1.","A clear zone is label 4.","The E. coli plate is label 2.","Lactose fermentation is read on CLED."],"k":"No zone around a disc = resistant.","f":"lm14","rn":[["","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone: sensitive; Growth up to disc: resistant"],["","Why does a clear zone form around an antibiotic disc?","Why does a clear zone form around an antibiotic disc?","Antibiotic diffuses into agar; Inhibits bacterial growth"]]},{"id":40022,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","a":["zone of inhibition","zoi","zone of inhibition diameter"],"s":"What is the clear area around an antibiotic disc where bacterial growth is stopped called?","e":"The zone of inhibition (ZOI) is the clear area around a disc where the antibiotic stops growth. Its diameter is measured and compared with standards to classify sensitive or resistant.","k":"ZOI = zone of inhibition.","rn":[["","How is antibiotic susceptibility measured on disc testing?","How is antibiotic susceptibility measured on disc testing?","Diameter of zone of inhibition"]]},{"id":40023,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of lab technique and the lecturer's comment is correct?","o":["Molecular techniques (PCR): most accurate","Microscopy: technically challenging and slow","Tissue culture: easy and quick","Serology: detects bacterial DNA","Culture: works for every organism easily"],"e":"The overview table: microscopy (seeing cells, bacteria, parasites; easy and quick); culture on nutrient and selective agar (bacteria that grow; pretty easy but some organisms are tricky); serology (difficult-to-culture organisms; detects antibodies in blood); molecular techniques (difficult-to-culture organisms; most accurate); tissue culture (intracellular organisms; technically challenging).","w":["Microscopy is easy and quick.","Tissue culture is technically challenging.","Serology detects antibodies.","Some organisms are really tricky to culture."],"k":"Microscopy quick; culture easy-ish; serology antibodies; PCR most accurate; tissue culture hard.","rn":[["","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","Serological techniques; Molecular techniques (PCR); Tissue culture"]]},{"id":40024,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which techniques are listed for difficult-to-culture organisms?","o":["Serology, molecular techniques and tissue culture","Microscopy only","Nutrient agar culture only","Urine dip-stick testing only, done in the GP's own office","Disc susceptibility testing only"],"e":"The overview table lists serological techniques, molecular techniques (PCR) and tissue culture as approaches for difficult-to-culture organisms. Microscopy and culture are for organisms you can see and grow.","w":["Microscopy is for seeing cells, bacteria and parasites.","Agar culture needs organisms that grow.","Dip-sticks screen urine but do not identify difficult organisms.","Disc testing needs a grown culture."],"k":"Difficult organisms: serology, PCR, tissue culture.","rn":[["","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","Serological techniques; Molecular techniques (PCR); Tissue culture"]]},{"id":40025,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which statement about the Gram stain is consistent with the lecture?","o":["It is quick, easy and very cheap","It takes several days and is very expensive","It identifies viruses directly","It is the best stain for TB","It only works on non-sterile samples"],"e":"Looking at a sample under the microscope takes about 10 seconds. The Gram stain is quick (the lecturer guessed 10–20 minutes), easy and very cheap. From urine you can classify Gram-positive vs Gram-negative and start counting white cells. TB needs ZN or auramine stains.","w":["It is quick and cheap.","Viruses need EM.","TB stains poorly with Gram.","It is most useful from sterile sites."],"k":"Gram stain: quick, easy, cheap.","rn":[["","Why is Gram staining useful in the lab?","Why is Gram staining useful in the lab?","Quick, easy, cheap; Gram positive or negative"]]},{"id":40026,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A patient with suprapubic pain and dysuria has a urine Gram stain showing polymorphonuclear cells and Gram-negative rods. What do the rods most likely represent?","o":["Coliforms (Enterobacteriaceae)","Gram-positive cocci in clusters, such as S. aureus","Acid-fast mycobacteria","Pinworm ova","Herpes virus particles"],"e":"Unstained urine allows white cells to be counted. The Gram stain shows polymorphonuclear cells (neutrophils, granulocytes) and Gram-negative rods, approximately coliforms (Enterobacteriaceae).","w":["The rods are Gram negative, not Gram-positive cocci.","Mycobacteria need ZN or auramine.","Pinworm ova come from the sellotape test.","Viruses need electron microscopy."],"k":"Gram-negative rods in urine ≈ coliforms.","f":"lm19","rn":[["","Gram negative rods in urine suggest what?","Gram negative rods in urine suggest what?","Coliform (Enterobacteriaceae)"]]},{"id":40027,"t":"Case 1: Urinary Tract Infection","a":["dysuria"],"s":"What is the term for painful urination?","e":"Dysuria is painful urination. In the lecture, a patient with suprapubic pain and dysuria had polymorphonuclear cells and Gram-negative rods on urine Gram stain.","k":"Dysuria = painful urination.","rn":[["","What does dysuria mean?","What does dysuria mean?","Painful urination"]]},{"id":40028,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A child in the emergency department has fever, headache and photophobia. A lumbar puncture yields cloudy, turbid CSF. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Give antibiotics immediately and still send the sample to the lab","Wait 48 hours for culture before any treatment","Send the patient home with advice","Treat only if the Gram stain, sent to the lab, later shows bacteria in the CSF","Repeat the lumbar puncture after one week"],"e":"Fever, headache and photophobia suggest meningitis. Cloudy, turbid CSF indicates potential bacterial infection (high probability, not proof). Bacterial meningitis can kill within hours, so you give antibiotics straight away rather than waiting 48 hours for results, and still send the sample to the lab. Being wrong does not matter; you may have saved a life.","w":["Waiting 48 hours could be fatal.","This would be dangerous.","Treatment should not wait for the Gram stain.","Delay could be fatal."],"k":"Suspected bacterial meningitis: antibiotics immediately, then confirm.","rn":[["","What is the first action with turbid CSF in a sick child?","What is the first action with turbid CSF in a sick child?","Give antibiotics straight away"]]},{"id":40029,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"CSF Gram stain shows Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils. Which organism is most likely?","o":["Neisseria meningitidis","Streptococcus pneumoniae, which forms chains","Staphylococcus aureus, which forms clusters","Escherichia coli, a Gram-negative rod","Mycobacterium tuberculosis, which is acid-fast"],"e":"In the meningitis case, the lab Gram stain showed intracellular Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils, approximately Neisseria meningitidis. Diplococci are cocci in pairs.","w":["S. pneumoniae is given as an example of streptococci (chains).","S. aureus forms clusters.","E. coli is a rod, not a diplococcus.","TB needs an acid-fast stain."],"k":"Gram-negative intracellular diplococci in CSF ≈ N. meningitidis.","f":"lm20","rn":[["","Image card: CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","Gram negative diplococci in neutrophils; Neisseria meningitidis"],["","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","Neisseria meningitidis"]]},{"id":40030,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Why are patients with suspected meningitis often given antibiotics even though the cause may be viral?","o":["Initial symptoms are similar, and bacterial meningitis can kill within hours","Antibiotics cure viral meningitis","Viral meningitis is always more serious","Lab tests can never distinguish viral from bacterial meningitis, so everyone is treated the same","Antibiotics prevent the need for a lumbar puncture"],"e":"Aseptic (viral) meningitis is usually benign, whereas bacterial meningitis is extremely serious and can kill within hours. The initial symptoms are similar, so patients are often given antibiotics while bacterial meningitis is ruled out or confirmed by lab testing.","w":["Antibiotics do not treat viruses; they cover possible bacterial meningitis.","Viral meningitis is usually less serious.","Lab testing is used to rule bacterial meningitis in or out.","The lumbar puncture is still done."],"k":"Cover for bacterial meningitis until the lab rules it out.","rn":[["","Why not wait for culture before treating suspected bacterial meningitis?","Why not wait for culture before treating suspected bacterial meningitis?","Culture takes ~48 hours; Can kill within hours"]]},{"id":40031,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"In which sample is a Gram stain most likely to give a clear, useful answer?","o":["Joint fluid from a hot, swollen knee","Sputum from a patient with a chest infection","A stool sample from a patient with diarrhoea","A swab from the surface of the skin","Saliva from the mouth"],"e":"A sterile site is one where you would not normally find bacteria, such as CSF and joint fluid (the notes also list blood, bone marrow, brain, heart, liver, spleen and kidney). Microscopy and Gram stains are useful there because a single pathogen is expected. At non-sterile sites, such as sputum, the sample is loaded with bacteria, debris and cells, so the Gram stain is often not useful.","w":["Sputum is non-sterile and full of mixed organisms.","Stool is a non-sterile sample with many bacteria.","Skin surfaces carry many commensals.","Saliva is a non-sterile sample."],"k":"Gram stain is most useful from sterile sites (CSF, joint fluid).","rn":[["","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Sterile sites; e.g. CSF, joint fluid"]]},{"id":40032,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A sputum Gram stain shows Gram-negative cocci, Gram-positive cocci in clusters and chains, and Gram-positive rods. What is the best interpretation?","o":["A mixed non-sterile sample, so the Gram stain is hard to interpret","A single pathogen only, so the antibiotic treatment can now be chosen with certainty","A sterile sample with contamination from the lab","Proof of tuberculosis infection","A viral infection seen directly on the stain"],"e":"Sputum from a chest infection is a non-sterile sample loaded with bacteria, debris and cells. The figure shows Gram-negative cocci, Gram-positive cocci in clusters, Gram-positive cocci in chains and Gram-positive rods together, which is why non-sterile samples are hard to interpret.","w":["Many organism types are present, not a single pathogen.","Sputum is not a sterile sample.","TB needs an acid-fast stain.","Viruses cannot be seen on a Gram stain."],"k":"Mixed flora in sputum makes Gram stains unhelpful.","f":"lm21","rn":[["","Why is a sputum Gram stain often not useful in a chest infection?","Why is a sputum Gram stain often not useful in a chest infection?","Non-sterile site; Full of bacteria, debris, cells"],["","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","Sample from sterile site; Single pathogen expected"]]},{"id":40033,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which statement about bacteria found in the blood is consistent with the lecture?","o":["It is a really bad sign, because blood is a sterile site","It is a normal finding, because blood is a non-sterile site that always carries bacteria","It is expected after every meal","It means only that the sample was contaminated","It never happens in humans"],"e":"Sterile sites, where bacteria are not normally found, include CSF and joint fluid on the slide, and blood, bone marrow, brain, heart, liver, spleen and kidney in the notes. Bacteria found in blood are a really bad sign.","w":["Blood is a sterile site.","Bacteria in blood are not expected after meals.","It is treated as a serious finding.","Bacteria can enter the blood."],"k":"Bacteria in blood (a sterile site) is a bad sign.","rn":[["","Which 3 fluids/tissues are sterile sites?","Which 3 fluids/tissues are sterile sites?","Blood; CSF; Bone marrow"],["","What is a sterile site?","What is a sterile site?","Normally no bacteria"],["","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Sterile sites; e.g. CSF, joint fluid"]]},{"id":40034,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of bacterial shape and name is correct?","o":["Comma-shaped: vibrio","Corkscrew: coccus","Spherical: bacillus","Rod: spirochaete","Spiral: coccobacillus"],"e":"Bacteria are classified by basic shape into spherical (cocci), rod (bacilli), spiral (spirilla), comma (vibrios) and corkscrew (spirochaetes); the slide also shows coccobacilli (short, stubby rods). The lecturer expects you to learn the names.","w":["Corkscrew shapes are spirochaetes.","Spherical shapes are cocci.","Rods are bacilli.","Spirals are spirilla; coccobacilli are short stubby rods."],"k":"Cocci spheres, bacilli rods, vibrios commas, spirilla spirals, spirochaetes corkscrews.","rn":[["","Bacterial shapes: which 3 curved shapes?","Bacterial shapes: which 3 curved shapes?","Spiral (spirilla); Comma (vibrios); Corkscrew (spirochaetes)"],["","What are the 2 types of spiral-shaped bacteria (not spirochaetes)?","What are the 2 types of spiral-shaped bacteria (not spirochaetes)?","Vibrios (comma-shaped); Spirilla"]]},{"id":40035,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Cocci arranged in grape-like clusters are given which name?","o":["Staphylococci","Streptococci","Diplococci, arranged in pairs","Tetracocci","Sarcine"],"e":"Cocci arrangements: micrococci (single), diplococci (pairs), streptococci (chains), staphylococci (clusters, grape-like), tetracocci (groups of four) and sarcine (cube-like packets). The names describe the arrangement: diplo = 2, strepto = chain, staphylo = cluster, tetra = 4.","w":["Streptococci form chains.","Diplococci form pairs.","Tetracocci form groups of four.","Sarcine form cube-like packets."],"k":"Staphylococci = clusters.","rn":[["","Diplococci vs streptococci vs staphylococci: arrangement?","Diplococci vs streptococci vs staphylococci: arrangement?","Diplo: pairs; Strepto: chains; Staphylo: clusters"],["","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Diplococci; Streptococci; Staphylococci"]]},{"id":40036,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of pathogen and coccal arrangement is correct?","o":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae: diplococci","Streptococcus pneumoniae: clusters","Staphylococcus aureus: chains","Neisseria gonorrhoeae: cube-like packets","Staphylococcus aureus: pairs"],"e":"The pathogenic examples: Neisseria gonorrhoeae as diplococci, Streptococcus pneumoniae as streptococci (chains) and Staphylococcus aureus as staphylococci (clusters).","w":["S. pneumoniae forms chains.","S. aureus forms clusters.","N. gonorrhoeae forms pairs.","S. aureus forms clusters."],"k":"N. gonorrhoeae pairs; S. pneumoniae chains; S. aureus clusters.","f":"lm24","rn":[["","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","Neisseria meningitidis"],["","Image card: CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","Gram negative diplococci in neutrophils; Neisseria meningitidis"]]},{"id":40037,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","a":["diplococci","diplococcus"],"s":"What term describes cocci arranged in pairs?","e":"Diplococci are cocci in pairs (diplo = 2). Neisseria gonorrhoeae and, in CSF, Neisseria meningitidis are seen as diplococci.","k":"Diplococci = pairs.","rn":[["","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","What does Gram negative diplococci in CSF suggest?","Neisseria meningitidis"],["","Image card: CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","Gram negative diplococci in neutrophils; Neisseria meningitidis"],["","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Diplococci; Streptococci; Staphylococci"]]},{"id":40038,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","a":["streptococci","streptococcus"],"s":"What term describes cocci arranged in chains?","e":"Streptococci are cocci in chains (strepto = chain). Streptococcus pneumoniae is the pathogenic example given.","k":"Streptococci = chains.","rn":[["","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Cocci arrangements: which 3 are pairs/chains/clusters?","Diplococci; Streptococci; Staphylococci"],["","Diplococci vs streptococci vs staphylococci: arrangement?","Diplococci vs streptococci vs staphylococci: arrangement?","Diplo: pairs; Strepto: chains; Staphylo: clusters"]]},{"id":40039,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which group of rod-shaped bacteria is labelled as producing spores?","o":["Clostridia, such as C. tetani and C. botulinum","Enteric rods, such as E. coli, Salmonella and Shigella","Vibrios, such as V. cholerae","Spirochaetes, such as Treponema","Diplococci, such as Neisseria"],"e":"The rod table lists enteric rods (E. coli, Salmonella, Shigella) as not producing spores, bacilli (B. anthracis; spores not stated on the slide) and clostridia (C. tetani, C. botulinum) as producing spores. Clostridium is a genus of Gram-positive bacteria including the causes of botulism and tetanus.","w":["Enteric rods don't produce spores.","Vibrios are comma-shaped, not the spore-forming rods listed.","Spirochaetes are corkscrew-shaped.","Neisseria are cocci."],"k":"Clostridia are spore-forming rods.","rn":[["","What are the 3 groups of rod-shaped bacteria (with examples)?","What are the 3 groups of rod-shaped bacteria (with examples)?","E. coli, Salmonella, Shigella; Bacilli (B. anthracis); Clostridia (C. tetani, C. botulinum)"]]},{"id":40040,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A farmer who handles livestock develops a serious infection with a large Gram-positive rod found in soil, water and vegetation. Which organism is most likely?","o":["Bacillus anthracis","Escherichia coli","Vibrio cholerae","Treponema pallidum","Neisseria meningitidis"],"e":"Bacillus anthracis is a large Gram-positive rod causing anthrax and is exceptionally dangerous. It is found in soil, water and vegetation and infects cows, sheep and horses, which infect humans after contact with contaminated material.","w":["E. coli is an enteric Gram-negative rod.","V. cholerae is comma-shaped and causes cholera.","T. pallidum is a spirochaete.","N. meningitidis is a diplococcus."],"k":"B. anthracis: large Gram-positive rod, anthrax, from animals/soil.","rn":[["","Where is Bacillus anthracis found and which animals does it infect?","Where is Bacillus anthracis found and which animals does it infect?","Soil, water, vegetation; Cows, sheep, horses"],["","Bacillus anthracis: Gram stain and shape?","Bacillus anthracis: Gram stain and shape?","Large Gram positive rod"]]},{"id":40041,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of rod arrangement and appearance is correct?","o":["Streptobacillus: rods in a chain","Coccobacillus: long thin rods in clusters","Bacillus: rods in pairs only","Streptobacillus: comma-shaped rods","Coccobacillus: corkscrew rods"],"e":"Rod arrangements: bacillus (single rod), streptobacillus (rods in a chain) and coccobacillus (short, stubby rod).","w":["A coccobacillus is a short, stubby rod.","A bacillus is a single rod.","Comma shapes are vibrios.","Corkscrew shapes are spirochaetes."],"k":"Bacillus single; streptobacillus chain; coccobacillus short and stubby.","rn":[["","What is a bacillus arrangement vs a streptobacillus?","What is a bacillus arrangement vs a streptobacillus?","Bacillus: single rods; Streptobacillus: rods joined together"]]},{"id":40042,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of spiral or spirochaete organism and disease is correct?","o":["Borrelia burgdorferi: Lyme disease","Helicobacter pylori: rat-bite fever","Spirillum minus: stomach ulcers","Leptospira interrogans: cholera","Vibrio cholerae: Lyme disease"],"e":"Vibrio cholerae (comma-shaped) causes cholera; Spirillum minus is spread by rats and causes rat-bite fever (sodoku); Helicobacter pylori causes stomach ulcers; Treponema pallidum subspecies cause endemic syphilis; Leptospira interrogans causes leptospirosis; Borrelia burgdorferi causes Lyme disease.","w":["H. pylori causes stomach ulcers.","Spirillum minus causes rat-bite fever.","L. interrogans causes leptospirosis.","V. cholerae causes cholera."],"k":"B. burgdorferi → Lyme; H. pylori → ulcers; S. minus → rat-bite fever.","rn":[["","Which spirochaete causes Lyme disease?","Which spirochaete causes Lyme disease?","Borrelia burgdorferi"]]},{"id":40043,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A sewage worker develops a usually mild febrile illness that can progress to liver or kidney failure. Which spirochaete fits the lecture's description?","o":["Leptospira interrogans","Borrelia recurrentis, a spirochaete listed alongside B. burgdorferi","Treponema pallidum","Helicobacter pylori","Spirillum minus"],"e":"Leptospira interrogans causes leptospirosis, usually a mild febrile illness that may cause liver or kidney failure. Treponema pallidum subspecies cause endemic syphilis; Borrelia burgdorferi causes Lyme disease; H. pylori causes stomach ulcers; Spirillum minus causes rat-bite fever.","w":["B. recurrentis is listed among Borrelia, but leptospirosis fits this description.","T. pallidum subspecies cause syphilis.","H. pylori causes stomach ulcers.","S. minus causes rat-bite fever."],"k":"Leptospirosis: mild fever, may cause liver/kidney failure.","rn":[["","What illness does Leptospira interrogans cause?","What illness does Leptospira interrogans cause?","Leptospirosis; Usually mild fever"],["","What disease does Leptospira interrogans cause?","What disease does Leptospira interrogans cause?","Leptospirosis"]]},{"id":40044,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","a":["vibrio","vibrios"],"s":"What term describes comma-shaped bacteria such as the cause of cholera?","e":"Vibrios are comma-shaped bacteria. Vibrio cholerae causes cholera, which the lecturer called \"particularly nasty\".","k":"Vibrio = comma-shaped.","rn":[["","Which bacteria resemble a comma?","Which bacteria resemble a comma?","Vibrios"],["","What are the 2 types of spiral-shaped bacteria (not spirochaetes)?","What are the 2 types of spiral-shaped bacteria (not spirochaetes)?","Vibrios (comma-shaped); Spirilla"],["","Bacterial shapes: which 3 curved shapes?","Bacterial shapes: which 3 curved shapes?","Spiral (spirilla); Comma (vibrios); Corkscrew (spirochaetes)"]]},{"id":40045,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Why do Gram-positive bacteria stain purple and Gram-negative bacteria pink?","o":["Gram positives have a thick peptidoglycan wall; Gram negatives a thin layer plus an outer membrane","Gram positives have an outer lipid membrane; Gram negatives do not","Gram negatives have a thick peptidoglycan wall with no outer membrane; Gram positives have only a thin one","Both have identical walls and differ only in DNA","Gram positives contain mycolic acid"],"e":"Gram-positive bacteria have a thick, uniformly dense peptidoglycan wall with no outer lipid membrane and stain purple. Gram-negative bacteria have a thin peptidoglycan layer plus an outer membrane (and periplasmic space) and stain pink.","w":["The outer membrane belongs to Gram negatives.","This reverses the peptidoglycan thickness.","Their walls differ structurally.","Mycolic acid is in mycobacteria."],"k":"Gram +: thick peptidoglycan, purple; Gram −: thin + outer membrane, pink.","rn":[["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan layer?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan layer?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"]]},{"id":40046,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"In this cell wall diagram, which label shows the wall with an outer membrane, thin peptidoglycan and periplasmic space?","o":["4, the Gram-negative wall","2, the Gram-positive wall","1, Gram-positive bacteria stained purple","3, Gram-negative bacteria stained pink","None of the labels"],"e":"The figure shows 1 Gram-positive bacteria staining purple; 2 the Gram-positive wall, thick peptidoglycan over the plasma membrane; 3 Gram-negative bacteria staining pink; 4 the Gram-negative wall: outer membrane, thin peptidoglycan, periplasmic space and plasma membrane.","w":["The Gram-positive wall has thick peptidoglycan and no outer membrane.","Label 1 shows stained bacteria, not the wall layers.","Label 3 shows stained bacteria, not the wall layers.","Label 4 shows this wall."],"k":"Gram-negative wall: outer membrane, thin peptidoglycan, periplasm.","f":"lm29","rn":[["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan layer?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: peptidoglycan layer?","Positive: thick; Negative: thin"],["","Gram positive vs Gram negative: outer lipid membrane?","Gram positive vs Gram negative: outer lipid membrane?","Positive: none; Negative: present"]]},{"id":40047,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"An HIV-positive patient with pneumonia has a lung biopsy. Which stain and organism are described in the lecture?","o":["Silver stain; Pneumocystis jirovecii","Gram stain; Neisseria meningitidis","Ziehl-Neelsen; Pneumocystis jirovecii","Auramine; herpes virus","Carbolfuchsin; Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"e":"A silver stain of lung biopsy in an HIV-positive patient with pneumonia shows Pneumocystis jirovecii (previously P. carinii). The best diagnosis uses Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain on lung tissue or bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid.","w":["N. meningitidis is seen in CSF, not this lung biopsy.","ZN is for acid-fast mycobacteria.","Herpes virus is seen by electron microscopy of vesicle fluid.","Carbolfuchsin is the ZN primary stain."],"k":"Pneumocystis jirovecii: GMS silver stain on lung tissue or BAL.","rn":[["","Image card: Silver stain of lung biopsy, HIV positive patient with pneumonia: which organism?","Silver stain of lung biopsy, HIV positive patient with pneumonia: which organism?","Pneumocystis jirovecii (previously P. carinii)"],["","What samples is the Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain done on for Pneumocystis pneu…","What samples is the Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) stain done on for Pneumocystis pneumonia?","Lung tissue; Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid"]]},{"id":40048,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Fluid is collected from a vesicular rash. No bacteria are seen, but electron microscopy shows a virus. What is the most likely finding?","o":["Herpes virus, consistent with herpes zoster","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Gram-negative diplococci seen inside neutrophils in the vesicle fluid","Acid-fast bacilli","Pinworm ova"],"e":"Fluid from a vesicular rash (collected carefully) showed no bacteria, and an electron micrograph of vesicle fluid showed herpes virus, approximately herpes zoster, which is highly infectious. Electron microscopy lets you see viruses and still counts as microscopy.","w":["Pneumocystis is seen with silver stain in lung.","Diplococci are bacteria seen on Gram stain.","Acid-fast bacilli are mycobacteria.","Pinworm ova are seen on the sellotape test."],"k":"EM of vesicle fluid shows herpes virus.","f":"lm30","rn":[["","Image card: Electron micrograph of vesicle fluid from a vesicular rash: which virus?","Electron micrograph of vesicle fluid from a vesicular rash: which virus?","Herpes virus (Herpes zoster)"]]},{"id":40049,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Why do mycobacteria such as TB not stain well with the Gram stain?","o":["Their wall contains waxy mycolic acid","They have no cell wall at all","They are too small to be seen with any microscope","They are viruses, not bacteria","They dissolve in crystal violet"],"e":"TB (mycobacteria) do not stain well with the Gram stain, so the Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) or auramine fluorescence stain is used. Mycobacteria are acid-fast because their wall contains mycolic acid: waxy, it stops the stain leaving through the wall.","w":["They do have a cell wall, with a thick mycolic acid layer.","They can be seen with special stains.","TB is a bacterium.","The problem is the waxy wall, not dissolution."],"k":"TB: waxy mycolic acid → poor Gram stain → use ZN or auramine.","rn":[["","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Waxy mycolic acid wall; Retains the stain"]]},{"id":40050,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Put the Ziehl-Neelsen steps in order: (P) counterstain with methylene blue; (Q) apply carbolfuchsin; (R) decolourise with acid alcohol; (S) heat fix to the slide.","o":["Q → S → R → P","S → Q → P → R","Q → R → S → P","P → Q → S → R","Q → S → P → R"],"e":"ZN steps: apply primary stain carbolfuchsin for 30 s (bacteria turn pink); heat fix cells to the slide using a flame; decolourise with acid alcohol for 15–20 s (TB keeps the pink; others lose it); counterstain with methylene blue for 30 s, then rinse. Non-acid-fast organisms appear blue.","w":["Carbolfuchsin is applied first.","Decolourising comes after heat fixing.","Methylene blue counterstain is the last step.","Decolourising must come before the counterstain."],"k":"ZN: carbolfuchsin → heat fix → acid alcohol → methylene blue.","rn":[["","Ziehl-Neelsen: last 2 steps?","Ziehl-Neelsen: last 2 steps?","Acid alcohol 15-20 s; Methylene blue 30 s, rinse"]]},{"id":40051,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"After a Ziehl-Neelsen stain, what colours are acid-fast and non-acid-fast organisms?","o":["Acid-fast pink-red; non-acid-fast blue","Acid-fast blue; non-acid-fast pink","Both purple","Acid-fast organisms glow green and non-acid-fast organisms turn yellow","Both colourless"],"e":"In ZN staining, acid-fast organisms such as TB keep the pink-red carbolfuchsin after acid-alcohol decolourisation, while non-acid-fast organisms lose it and take up the blue methylene blue counterstain. Auramine instead makes TB glow green under fluorescence.","w":["This reverses the colours.","Purple is Gram-positive staining.","Green fluorescence is auramine, not ZN.","Both take up a stain."],"k":"ZN: TB pink-red on a blue background.","f":"lm31","rn":[["","Ziehl-Neelsen: acid-fast vs non-acid-fast colour?","Ziehl-Neelsen: acid-fast vs non-acid-fast colour?","Acid-fast: pink; Non-acid-fast: blue"]]},{"id":40052,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Which statement about auramine staining compared with Ziehl-Neelsen is correct?","o":["Auramine is fluorescent and more sensitive and rapid than ZN","Auramine is less sensitive and slower than ZN, so it is used only as a backup stain","Auramine works only for Gram-negative bacteria","Auramine cannot stain mycobacteria","Auramine needs electron microscopy"],"e":"Auramine phenol stain is fluorescent; viewed under laser light it works the same way as ZN, being trapped by mycolic acid. It is more sensitive and rapid than ZN, and TB appears as glowing green bacilli.","w":["It is more sensitive and faster.","It is used for mycobacteria.","It does stain mycobacteria.","It uses fluorescence, not EM."],"k":"Auramine: fluorescent, more sensitive and faster than ZN.","rn":[["","Auramine phenol vs Ziehl-Neelsen for TB?","Auramine phenol vs Ziehl-Neelsen for TB?","Auramine more sensitive and rapid"]]},{"id":40053,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","a":["acid-fast","acid fast","acid-fastness"],"s":"What term describes bacteria that resist decolourisation by acid-alcohol after staining?","e":"Acid-fast bacteria resist decolourisation by acid-alcohol (e.g. 3% HCl, 95% ethanol) once stained. Mycobacteria are acid-fast because of waxy mycolic acid in their wall.","k":"Acid-fast = resists acid-alcohol decolourisation.","rn":[["","What does acid-fast mean?","What does acid-fast mean?","Resists acid-alcohol; Decolourisation once stained"]]},{"id":40054,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","a":["mycolic acid"],"s":"Name the waxy cell wall component that makes mycobacteria acid-fast.","e":"Mycobacteria are acid-fast because their wall contains mycolic acid, a waxy layer that stops the stain leaving through the wall.","k":"Mycolic acid makes mycobacteria acid-fast.","rn":[["","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Waxy mycolic acid wall; Retains the stain"]]},{"id":40055,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"In this diagram of the mycobacterial cell wall, which layer traps the stain and makes the organism acid-fast?","o":["The waxy mycolic acid layer","The cytoplasmic membrane","The porins","The surface proteins","The peptidoglycan layer alone, as in Gram-positive bacteria"],"e":"The acid-fast cell wall figure labels surface proteins, acyl lipids, porins, the waxy mycolic acid layer, arabinogalactan, lipoarabinomannan, peptidoglycan, PIM and the cytoplasmic membrane. The thick mycolic acid layer is what traps the stain.","w":["The cytoplasmic membrane is the innermost layer.","Porins are channels in the wall.","Surface proteins are outermost.","Peptidoglycan is present but the mycolic acid traps the stain."],"k":"Mycolic acid layer traps stain in mycobacteria.","f":"lmw31","rn":[["","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Waxy mycolic acid wall; Retains the stain"],["","Which 3 layers lie outside the mycobacterial membrane?","Which 3 layers lie outside the mycobacterial membrane?","Peptidoglycan; Arabinogalactan; Mycolic acid"]]},{"id":40056,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"A child at school has an itchy anus at night, and parents also develop symptoms. Which diagnostic test fits the lecture?","o":["The sellotape test, looking for pinworm ova under the microscope","A blood culture on CLED agar","A Ziehl-Neelsen stain of sputum","PCR for herpes simplex virus on a swab from the perianal skin","An API strip of the stool"],"e":"Pinworms (Enterobius vermicularis) are common in children and schools and very infectious; itch from anal inflammation leads to scratching and hand-to-nose transmission, so parents often get them too. The sellotape test: apply tape (wearing gloves) to the affected area, pull it off, and microscopy shows the ova; send to the lab for diagnosis.","w":["CLED is for urine bacteria.","ZN is for TB.","HSV PCR is not relevant.","API strips identify Gram-negative bacteria by metabolism."],"k":"Pinworm: sellotape test shows ova.","rn":[["","Image card: Sellotape test microscopy from a child with an itchy anus: what is seen?","Sellotape test microscopy from a child with an itchy anus: what is seen?","Ova of pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis)"],["","What does Sellotape test microscopy show?","What does Sellotape test microscopy show?","Ova (eggs) of pinworm"]]},{"id":40057,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","a":["pinworm","pinworms","enterobius vermicularis","enterobius","threadworm"],"s":"Name the tiny, white worm that causes anal itching in children and is diagnosed with the sellotape test.","e":"Pinworms (Enterobius vermicularis) are tiny, narrow, white and less than half an inch long. They are diagnosed by the sellotape test, which shows the ova under the microscope.","k":"Pinworm = Enterobius vermicularis.","rn":[["","Image card: Sellotape test microscopy from a child with an itchy anus: what is seen?","Sellotape test microscopy from a child with an itchy anus: what is seen?","Ova of pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis)"],["","What test diagnoses pinworm?","What test diagnoses pinworm?","Sellotape test"],["","What parasite causes an itchy anus in children?","What parasite causes an itchy anus in children?","Pinworm (Enterobius vermicularis)"]]},{"id":40058,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Which pairing of microscopy method and use is correct?","o":["Electron microscopy: viruses","Gram staining: parasites and eggs","Direct visualisation: TB","Special stains: samples from sterile sites only","Electron microscopy: counting urine white cells"],"e":"Microscopy summary: Gram staining for bacteria, especially from sterile sites with a single expected pathogen; special stains for other organisms such as TB; electron microscopy for viruses; direct visualisation for parasites and eggs.","w":["Parasites and eggs are directly visualised.","TB needs special stains.","Special stains are for organisms like TB.","Urine WBCs are counted on simple microscopy."],"k":"Gram: bacteria; special stains: TB; EM: viruses; direct: parasites.","rn":[["","Microscopy uses: 2 other uses?","Microscopy uses: 2 other uses?","EM for viruses; See parasites and eggs"]]},{"id":40059,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Which statement about using microscopy for viruses is correct?","o":["Electron microscopy can show virus particles and still counts as microscopy","Light microscopy with a Gram stain shows virus particles clearly in most samples","Viruses stain pink with Ziehl-Neelsen","Viruses can only be detected with the sellotape test","Viruses grow as golden colonies on blood agar"],"e":"EM lets you see viruses and still counts as microscopy, as with herpes virus in vesicle fluid. Gram staining does not show viruses, ZN is for acid-fast bacteria, the sellotape test is for pinworm, and viruses do not grow on agar.","w":["Gram stain does not show viruses.","ZN is for mycobacteria.","The sellotape test is for pinworm.","Viruses do not grow on agar."],"k":"EM visualises viruses.","rn":[["","What test shows the virus in vesicle fluid from a vesicular rash?","What test shows the virus in vesicle fluid from a vesicular rash?","Electron microscopy"]]},{"id":40060,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"A doctor wants to diagnose TB from a sputum sample quickly and with high sensitivity. Which staining approach is best according to the lecture?","o":["Auramine fluorescence stain","Gram stain","Silver (GMS) stain","Electron microscopy","Chocolate agar culture"],"e":"TB does not stain well with the Gram stain. Ziehl-Neelsen and auramine both rely on mycolic acid trapping the stain, and auramine is more sensitive and rapid than ZN. Silver stain is for Pneumocystis, EM for viruses, and culture is slow.","w":["Gram staining works poorly for TB.","GMS silver is for Pneumocystis jirovecii.","EM is for viruses.","Culture is slow and not a staining method."],"k":"Auramine: sensitive, rapid TB stain.","rn":[["","What are the 2 stains for tuberculosis?","What are the 2 stains for tuberculosis?","Ziehl-Neelsen stain; Auramine fluorescence stain"],["","Image card: Which TB stain is which? (left vs right)","Which TB stain is which? (left vs right)","Left: Ziehl-Neelsen; Right: Auramine fluorescence"]]},{"id":40061,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as information provided by bacterial culture?","o":["Detection of antibodies in the patient's serum","Quantifying how many bacteria are present in the original sample","Purification","Provisional identification","Antibiotic susceptibility testing"],"e":"Culture is slow but provides a lot of information: quantifying bacteria, purification, provisional identification and antibiotic susceptibility testing. Detecting antibodies in serum is serology, used for difficult-to-culture organisms.","w":["Quantification is listed.","Purification is listed.","Provisional identification is listed.","Susceptibility testing is listed."],"k":"Culture: quantify, purify, identify, test susceptibility.","rn":[["","What is serology in practice?","What is serology in practice?","Detecting antibodies in serum"]]},{"id":40062,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"How are bacteria traditionally identified beyond appearance in the lab?","o":["By their metabolism, using biochemical assays","By counting white cells in the sample","By measuring the patient's temperature","By the colour of the patient's urine","By the size of the agar plate"],"e":"Identification is traditionally based on bacterial metabolism: each species metabolises slightly differently. A biochemical assay of the bacterial culture identifies the bacteria beyond \"it looks like E. coli\".","w":["WBC counts indicate infection, not species.","Temperature does not identify bacteria.","Urine colour does not identify species.","Plate size is irrelevant."],"k":"Bacteria are identified by their metabolism."},{"id":40063,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A Gram-negative isolate is tested on an API strip of up to 20 miniature biochemical tests, and the result pattern is entered into software. What happens next?","o":["The computer names the organism with a probability, e.g. E. coli 99.5%","The strip measures the zone of inhibition around each of its wells to give resistance","The strip shows a cytopathic effect","The strip detects IgM in serum","The strip amplifies DNA by thermal cycling"],"e":"API (analytical profile index) strips contain up to 20 miniature biochemical tests (different sugars such as lactose and sucrose, different pHs). The sample is added to each well, each shows positive or negative, and the results are entered into software linked to an online database, which names the organism, e.g. E. coli at 99.5% probability in the lecture video.","w":["Zones of inhibition are read on disc plates.","Cytopathic effect is seen in tissue culture.","IgM detection is serology.","Thermal cycling is PCR."],"k":"API: biochemical pattern → database → organism + probability.","rn":[["","Image card: Urine culture at 24 hours: what does the yellow colour show? Which organism is typical?","Urine culture at 24 hours: what does the yellow colour show? Which organism is typical?","Lactose fermentation (acid); E. coli"]]},{"id":40064,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"How accurate is metabolic profiling alone, and how can strain identification be improved?","o":["About 95% confidence; adding genetic profiling gives ~100%","About 50% confidence; adding Gram staining gives 100%","100% confidence with no further testing needed","About 10% confidence; it is rarely used","About 95%; adding a dip-stick gives 100%"],"e":"Metabolic profiling alone gives about 95% confidence. Adding genetic profiling identifies the strain accurately (~100%).","w":["The figure given is about 95%, and Gram staining does not reach 100%.","Metabolic profiling is not 100%.","It is widely used with about 95% confidence.","A dip-stick does not identify strains."],"k":"API ~95%; add genetics for strain-level ~100%.","rn":[["","How accurately can metabolic profiling identify bacteria?","How accurately can metabolic profiling identify bacteria?","About 95% confidence; Genetic profiling can reach 100%"]]},{"id":40065,"t":"Bacterial Culture","a":["api","analytical profile index","api strip","api test"],"s":"Name the standardised strip test with up to 20 miniature biochemical tests used to identify Gram-negative bacteria.","e":"The API (analytical profile index) test uses standardised strips with up to 20 miniature biochemical tests, with results matched against extensive databases.","k":"API = analytical profile index.","rn":[["","What does API stand for?","What does API stand for?","Analytical profile index"],["","Why are API (analytical profile index) strips useful?","Why are API (analytical profile index) strips useful?","Accurate identifications from extensive databases; Standardised, easy-to-use"],["","How many tests are on an API strip?","How many tests are on an API strip?","Up to 20"]]},{"id":40066,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"In this image, what is shown at label 2?","o":["Software that matches the result pattern to a database","An API 20 E strip with colour changes","A zone of inhibition around a disc","An agarose gel with PCR bands","A cytopathic effect in cell culture"],"e":"The figure shows 1 an API 20 E strip with its miniature tests (colour changes = positive/negative) and 2 software (apiweb) that matches the result pattern to the database.","w":["The strip is label 1.","Zones of inhibition are on disc plates.","Gels are used for PCR.","CPE is seen in tissue culture."],"k":"API results are matched by software to a database.","f":"lm37","rn":[["","How are API results read?","How are API results read?","Software linked to online database"]]},{"id":40067,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A doctor suspects bacterial meningitis in an emergency. Which limitation of culture is most relevant?","o":["It is slow, taking 24–48 h, so treatment cannot wait for it","It cannot detect any bacteria at all","It gives no information about antibiotic resistance, only about the shape of the bacteria","It works only for viruses","It is completely automated and needs no staff"],"e":"Culture-based diagnostics are good for detecting bacteria you can stain and grow and reliable for identification and resistance testing, but slow (24–48 h) and labour intensive. In an emergency such as possible bacterial meningitis you cannot wait for it.","w":["Culture detects bacteria that grow.","Culture is reliable for resistance testing.","Culture is for bacteria that grow, not viruses.","Culture is labour intensive."],"k":"Culture is slow (24–48 h) and labour intensive.","rn":[["","Why not wait for culture before treating suspected bacterial meningitis?","Why not wait for culture before treating suspected bacterial meningitis?","Culture takes ~48 hours; Can kill within hours"]]},{"id":40068,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"Why is culture described as labour intensive?","o":["Several people are needed to set up agar, run metabolic testing and handle many samples","It requires one automated robot that runs every test from plating to identification","It needs a surgeon to collect every sample","It requires electron microscopy for every plate","It must be repeated every hour for a week"],"e":"Culture is labour intensive: several people are involved, from a technician setting up the right agar to metabolic testing, across many samples.","w":["It is not described as a single-robot process.","Most samples are not collected by surgeons.","EM is not used for culture plates.","Hourly repetition for a week is not described."],"k":"Culture: technicians, agar choice, metabolic tests, many samples.","rn":[["","Why is bacterial culture labour intensive?","Why is bacterial culture labour intensive?","Sample on correct agar; Then metabolic testing; Several people"]]},{"id":40069,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Why are viruses difficult to culture in the lab?","o":["They kill the cells you are trying to grow them in","They grow too quickly on agar","They are always too large to fit in cells","They only grow in soil","They stain too strongly with Gram stain"],"e":"Organisms that stain poorly or cannot be cultured include viruses, which are a pain to culture because they kill the cells you are trying to grow them in, and cell-wall-deficient bacteria.","w":["Viruses do not grow on agar.","Viruses are smaller than cells.","Viruses need living cells.","Viruses are not seen with Gram stain."],"k":"Viruses kill the cells used to culture them.","rn":[["","Why are viruses hard to culture?","Why are viruses hard to culture?","Kill the cells; They grow in"]]},{"id":40070,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"An older patient has fever, cough, chest pain and confusion: community-acquired pneumonia. Sputum Gram stain and culture are negative. Which organisms might have been missed?","o":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae, which culture easily","Escherichia coli and Salmonella","Neisseria meningitidis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Staphylococcus aureus and S. epidermidis"],"e":"CAP causes: Streptococcus pneumoniae (~30%) and Haemophilus influenzae (~10%) Gram stain and culture easily; Mycoplasma pneumoniae (~30%) and Chlamydia pneumoniae (~10%) Gram stain poorly and are difficult to culture. About 40% of CAP would be missed by Gram stain and culture, so other methods are needed.","w":["These two Gram stain and culture easily.","These are not the CAP organisms listed.","These cause meningitis and gonorrhoea.","These are not the difficult CAP organisms listed."],"k":"Mycoplasma and Chlamydia (~40% of CAP) evade Gram stain and culture.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"]]},{"id":40071,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"What percentage of community-acquired pneumonia is caused by organisms that Gram stain poorly and are difficult to culture?","o":["About 40%","About 10%","About 70%","About 1%","About 100%"],"e":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae (~30%) and Chlamydia pneumoniae (~10%) together cause about 40% of CAP and would be missed by Gram stain and culture. S. pneumoniae (~30%) and H. influenzae (~10%) stain and culture easily.","w":["10% is Chlamydia alone.","70% is too high.","1% is far too low.","Some CAP organisms culture easily."],"k":"~40% of CAP missed by Gram/culture.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"],["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram stain and culture easily?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram stain and culture easily?","Streptococcus pneumoniae; Haemophilus influenzae"]]},{"id":40072,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","a":["mycoplasma pneumoniae","mycoplasma"],"s":"Name the organism causing about 30% of community-acquired pneumonia that Gram stains poorly and is difficult to culture.","e":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae causes about 30% of CAP; it Gram stains poorly and is difficult to culture, so serology or molecular methods are needed.","k":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae: ~30% of CAP, hard to culture.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"],["","Community-acquired pneumonia: 2 causes at ~30%?","Community-acquired pneumonia: 2 causes at ~30%?","S. pneumoniae; Mycoplasma pneumoniae"]]},{"id":40073,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"What does serology detect in practice?","o":["Antibodies in the serum","Bacterial colonies on agar","Viral DNA amplified by thermal cycling","Acid-fast bacilli on a slide","Lactose fermentation on CLED"],"e":"Serology (\"serum-ology\") is the study of serum and other body fluids; in practice it means detecting antibodies in the serum (blood). Antibodies form in response to infection, other foreign proteins or one's own proteins.","w":["Colonies are seen in culture.","DNA amplification is PCR.","Acid-fast bacilli are seen with ZN.","Lactose fermentation is read on CLED."],"k":"Serology detects antibodies in serum.","rn":[["","What is serology in practice?","What is serology in practice?","Detecting antibodies in serum"]]},{"id":40074,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Which statement about IgM and IgG in serology is correct?","o":["IgM indicates acute infection; IgG a long-standing response","IgG indicates acute infection; IgM a long-standing response","Both appear only years after infection","IgM is the most common antibody in all fluids","IgG is the first antibody made against a new infection"],"e":"IgM indicates acute infection: it is mainly in blood and lymph and is the first antibody made against a new infection. IgG indicates a long-standing immune response: it is the most common antibody, found in blood and other fluids, and takes time to form after infection or immunisation.","w":["This reverses the meanings.","IgM appears early.","IgG is the most common antibody.","IgM is the first antibody made."],"k":"IgM = acute; IgG = long-standing.","rn":[["","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM: acute infection; IgG: long-standing immune response"]]},{"id":40075,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"On this graph of antibodies over time after antigen exposure, which line rises and falls first?","o":["IgM (red)","IgG (green), which rises later and stays high","Both lines rise at exactly the same time","Neither line changes","IgE (blue)"],"e":"After antigen exposure, IgM (red) rises and falls first; IgG (green) rises later and stays high.","w":["IgG rises later and stays high.","IgM rises first.","Both lines change.","IgE is not plotted."],"k":"IgM rises first; IgG later and lasts.","f":"lm42","rn":[["","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM: acute infection; IgG: long-standing immune response"],["","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Acute EBV infection"],["","IgM positive, IgG negative means what?","IgM positive, IgG negative means what?","Acute infection"]]},{"id":40076,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"An 18-year-old student has fever, sore throat and swollen neck lymph glands. First serology: EBV IgM negative, EBV IgG negative. What does this mean?","o":["She is EBV susceptible (never infected)","She has an acute EBV infection that has only just started","She had EBV in the past and is immune","She has acute CMV infection","The test proves she has tonsillitis"],"e":"EBV IgM and IgG both negative means EBV susceptible: never infected. Antibodies take time to develop, so a repeat test a week later is needed. A week later her EBV IgM was positive, meaning acute EBV infection.","w":["Acute EBV needs a positive IgM.","Past infection would show IgG.","The CMV results show past CMV infection, not acute.","Serology does not prove tonsillitis."],"k":"IgM− IgG− = never infected (susceptible).","rn":[["","IgM negative, IgG negative means what?","IgM negative, IgG negative means what?","Susceptible (never infected)"]]},{"id":40077,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"In the same student, CMV IgM is negative and CMV IgG is positive. How is this interpreted?","o":["CMV immune: infected in the past","Acute CMV infection","Never infected with CMV","Acute EBV infection","The test failed"],"e":"CMV IgM negative and IgG positive means CMV immune: infected in the past. IgM indicates acute infection, IgG a long-standing response. Her repeat EBV IgM became positive, showing acute EBV.","w":["Acute CMV would show positive IgM.","A positive IgG shows past infection.","These results concern CMV, not EBV.","The pattern is interpretable."],"k":"IgM− IgG+ = past infection, immune.","rn":[["","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","Immune (past infection)"]]},{"id":40078,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"A week later the student's EBV IgM is positive and EBV IgG still negative. What does this show, and what principle does it illustrate?","o":["Acute EBV infection; antibodies take time to develop, so a repeat test was needed","Past EBV infection, because IgM persists for life after glandular fever","Never infected; the first test was correct","Acute CMV infection; CMV and EBV are the same virus","Bacterial tonsillitis; serology detects bacteria"],"e":"EBV IgM becoming positive a week later means acute EBV infection (glandular fever). The case illustrates that serology diagnoses viral infections, is fairly quick, can pinpoint the cause, and that antibodies take time to develop, hence the repeat test a week later. Serology is also used for Chlamydia and Mycoplasma.","w":["IgM indicates acute, not past, infection.","The new IgM shows she has now been infected.","CMV results were unchanged (immune).","Here serology detected antibodies to a virus."],"k":"Rising IgM on repeat testing = acute infection; antibodies take time.","rn":[["","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Acute EBV infection"]]},{"id":40079,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Which clinical feature in the student's case pointed the class towards glandular fever rather than simple tonsillitis?","o":["Swollen lymph glands in the neck","A sore throat alone","A fever alone","Sweats alone","Being at the end of freshers' week"],"e":"The student had fever, sweats, a sore throat and swollen lymph glands in her neck. Class ideas were tonsillitis or glandular fever; the swollen neck lymph glands pointed to glandular fever, caused by EBV or CMV.","w":["A sore throat occurs in both.","Fever occurs in both.","Sweats are non-specific.","The timing is context, not the distinguishing sign."],"k":"Swollen neck nodes suggested glandular fever.","rn":[["","Fresher case (18): 2 throat/neck symptoms?","Fresher case (18): 2 throat/neck symptoms?","Sore throat; Swollen neck lymph glands"],["","Image card: 18-year-old fresher with fever, sweats, sore throat and swollen neck glands: what does …","18-year-old fresher with fever, sweats, sore throat and swollen neck glands: what does the GP suspect? Which 2 viruses?","Glandular fever; EBV, CMV"]]},{"id":40080,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Serology is used for which organisms besides viruses, according to the lecture?","o":["Chlamydia and Mycoplasma","Pinworm ova","Neisseria meningitidis in CSF","E. coli on CLED","Pneumocystis on silver stain"],"e":"Serology is used to diagnose viral infections and is also used for Chlamydia and Mycoplasma, organisms that Gram stain poorly and are difficult to culture.","w":["Pinworm is diagnosed with the sellotape test.","N. meningitidis is seen on Gram stain.","E. coli is cultured on CLED.","Pneumocystis is seen with silver stain."],"k":"Serology also diagnoses Chlamydia and Mycoplasma.","rn":[["","Serology is used to diagnose what?","Serology is used to diagnose what?","Viral infections; Chlamydia; Mycoplasma"]]},{"id":40081,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","a":["igm","immunoglobulin m"],"s":"Which antibody class is the first made against a new infection and indicates acute infection?","e":"IgM is the first antibody made against a new infection and indicates acute infection; it is found mainly in blood and lymph.","k":"IgM = first antibody, acute.","rn":[["","When is IgM made?","When is IgM made?","First antibody; In a new infection"]]},{"id":40082,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","a":["igg","immunoglobulin g"],"s":"Which antibody class is the most common and indicates a long-standing immune response?","e":"IgG is the most common antibody, found in blood and other fluids, and indicates a long-standing immune response; it takes time to form after infection or immunisation.","k":"IgG = long-standing.","rn":[["","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM: acute infection; IgG: long-standing immune response"]]},{"id":40083,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"A patient's serology shows IgG positive but IgM negative for a virus. Which interpretation is correct?","o":["Past infection; the patient is immune","Acute infection happening right now","Never infected and susceptible","The test is invalid","Bacterial infection"],"e":"IgM positive means acute (current) infection; IgG positive with IgM negative means past infection and immunity; both negative means never infected and susceptible.","w":["Acute infection would show IgM.","Never infected would show both negative.","The pattern is interpretable.","This is viral serology."],"k":"IgG+ IgM− = past infection, immune.","rn":[["","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","Immune (past infection)"]]},{"id":40084,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","a":["chlamydia pneumoniae","chlamydia"],"s":"Name the organism causing about 10% of community-acquired pneumonia that Gram stains poorly and is difficult to culture.","e":"Chlamydia pneumoniae causes about 10% of CAP, Gram stains poorly and is difficult to culture; serology is also used for Chlamydia.","k":"Chlamydia pneumoniae: ~10% of CAP, hard to culture.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"],["","Community-acquired pneumonia: 2 causes at ~10%?","Community-acquired pneumonia: 2 causes at ~10%?","H. influenzae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"]]},{"id":40085,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Which CAP organisms stain and culture easily?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae","Only Mycoplasma pneumoniae","None of the CAP organisms","Only Chlamydia pneumoniae"],"e":"In the CAP table, Streptococcus pneumoniae (~30%) and Haemophilus influenzae (~10%) Gram stain and culture easily; Mycoplasma pneumoniae (~30%) and Chlamydia pneumoniae (~10%) Gram stain poorly and are difficult to culture.","w":["These two are the difficult ones.","Mycoplasma is difficult to culture.","S. pneumoniae and H. influenzae culture easily.","Chlamydia is difficult to culture."],"k":"S. pneumoniae and H. influenzae culture easily.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram stain and culture easily?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram stain and culture easily?","Streptococcus pneumoniae; Haemophilus influenzae"]]},{"id":40086,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A plate packed with many different antibiotic discs is used. What is measured around each disc?","o":["The zone of inhibition diameter","The number of colonies inside each disc","The colour change from yellow to blue","The size of the bacteria under EM","The temperature of the plate"],"e":"Disc testing as in case 1, but a plate can be packed with many different antibiotic discs. The zone of inhibition (ZOI) diameter around each disc is measured and compared with standards.","w":["Colonies are not counted inside discs.","Colour changes are read on CLED.","EM is not used here.","Temperature is not the readout."],"k":"Measure ZOI around each disc.","f":"lm36","rn":[["","How is antibiotic susceptibility measured on disc testing?","How is antibiotic susceptibility measured on disc testing?","Diameter of zone of inhibition"]]},{"id":40087,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"Which statement about what grows on an agar plate is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Anything infecting the sample grows; even breathing on a plate grows something","Only one organism can ever grow on a plate","Viruses grow best on agar","Agar kills all bacteria","Nothing grows without antibiotics"],"e":"Culture: squirt a sample (e.g. urine) on an agar plate and anything infecting it grows; the lecturer noted even breathing on a plate grows something. Viruses do not grow on agar, and antibiotics in agar are used selectively.","w":["Mixed growth is possible.","Viruses need living cells.","Agar supports bacterial growth.","Bacteria grow without antibiotics."],"k":"Agar grows whatever bacteria are present."},{"id":40088,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"Which of these is a strength of culture-based diagnostics?","o":["Reliable identification and resistance testing","Answers within minutes","No staff are required once the plate is set up","Detects all viruses","Works best for Mycoplasma"],"e":"Culture is good for detecting bacteria you can stain and grow and reliable for identification and resistance testing, but it is slow (24–48 h) and labour intensive.","w":["Culture takes 24–48 h.","It is labour intensive.","Viruses are difficult to culture.","Mycoplasma is difficult to culture."],"k":"Culture is reliable for ID and resistance testing.","rn":[["","What 2 things does culture do reliably?","What 2 things does culture do reliably?","Identification; Resistance testing"]]},{"id":40089,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Which organisms are named as difficult because they stain poorly or cannot be cultured?","o":["Viruses and cell-wall-deficient bacteria","E. coli and S. aureus","Lactose fermenters","All Gram-positive cocci","Fastidious respiratory pathogens on chocolate agar"],"e":"Organisms that stain poorly or cannot be cultured include viruses (which kill the cells you grow them in) and cell-wall-deficient bacteria, such as Mycoplasma.","w":["E. coli and S. aureus culture easily.","Lactose fermenters grow on CLED.","Gram-positive cocci stain well.","These grow on chocolate agar."],"k":"Viruses and cell-wall-deficient bacteria are hard to culture.","rn":[["","What 2 kinds of organism stain poorly or cannot be cultured?","What 2 kinds of organism stain poorly or cannot be cultured?","Viruses; Cell wall deficient bacteria"]]},{"id":40090,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"Why might serology be preferred over culture for suspected glandular fever?","o":["It diagnoses viral infections fairly quickly and can pinpoint the cause","It grows the virus on blood agar","It needs no blood sample","It shows bacteria directly","It is the only test that detects DNA"],"e":"Serology is used to diagnose viral infections; it is fairly quick and can pinpoint the cause, as in the EBV/CMV case. Viruses are difficult to culture because they kill their host cells.","w":["Viruses do not grow on agar.","Serology uses serum from blood.","Serology detects antibodies, not bacteria.","DNA detection is PCR."],"k":"Serology: quick, specific diagnosis of viral infection.","rn":[["","Serology is used to diagnose what?","Serology is used to diagnose what?","Viral infections; Chlamydia; Mycoplasma"],["","Which 4 herpes viruses cause viral encephalitis?","Which 4 herpes viruses cause viral encephalitis?","HSV; VZV; CMV; EBV"],["","Why treat suspected meningitis before results?","Why treat suspected meningitis before results?","Viral and bacterial look similar; Rule out bacterial quickly"]]},{"id":40091,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Which sequence of temperatures matches one PCR cycle?","o":["Denature 92–95 °C → anneal 50–70 °C → extend 72 °C","Anneal 92–95 °C → denature 50–70 °C → extend 72 °C","Extend 92–95 °C → anneal 72 °C → denature 50–70 °C","Denature 37 °C → anneal 4 °C → extend 121 °C","Denature 72 °C → anneal 92–95 °C → extend 50–70 °C"],"e":"One PCR cycle: (1) denaturation of the double-stranded template at 92–95 °C, melting it into single strands; (2) annealing of forward and reverse primers at 50–70 °C, depending on the melting temperature of the expected duplex; (3) extension at 72 °C for 0.5–3 min, as DNA polymerase extends from the primers' 3' ends.","w":["Annealing and denaturation temperatures are swapped.","The order and temperatures are scrambled.","These are not PCR temperatures.","Denaturation is the hottest step, not 72 °C."],"k":"PCR: denature 92–95, anneal 50–70, extend 72 °C.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of PCR?","What are the 3 steps of PCR?","Denaturation of dsDNA template; Annealing of primers; Extension of dsDNA molecules; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":40092,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"What is the role of primers in PCR?","o":["Short ssDNA \"launching platforms\" that bind complementary sequences for the polymerase","Enzymes that melt the double-stranded DNA template into single strands at 95 °C","Antibodies that detect viral proteins","Dyes that stain the gel","Proteins that kill cells in tissue culture"],"e":"In annealing, forward and reverse primers (short single-stranded DNA, easily bought online) bind complementary sequences of the template. They act as launching platforms from which DNA polymerase extends from the 3' end.","w":["Heat melts DNA in denaturation.","Antibodies are used in serology and immunofluorescence.","Ethidium bromide stains the gel.","Cell killing is a cytopathic effect."],"k":"Primers: short ssDNA starting points for polymerase.","rn":[["","What happens during annealing?","What happens during annealing?","Forward and reverse primers; Bind complementary sequences"],["","What is the job of a primer?","What is the job of a primer?","Launching platform for DNA polymerase"]]},{"id":40093,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Why is Taq polymerase used in PCR?","o":["It is thermally stable and does not denature at PCR temperatures","It works only at body temperature, 37 °C, and is destroyed by heat","It converts RNA into protein","It binds antibodies in serum","It produces lactic acid on CLED"],"e":"Taq DNA polymerase is thermally stable and does not denature under PCR temperatures. It was first found in Thermus aquaticus from hot (volcanic) springs. Newer thermostable polymerases also have proof-reading ability.","w":["It must survive much higher temperatures.","It copies DNA, not RNA into protein.","It does not bind antibodies.","Lactic acid comes from lactose fermentation."],"k":"Taq: heat-stable polymerase from hot-spring Thermus aquaticus.","rn":[["","Why is Taq used in PCR?","Why is Taq used in PCR?","Thermally stable; Does not denature"]]},{"id":40094,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","a":["thermus aquaticus","t aquaticus","t. aquaticus"],"s":"From which organism, found in hot springs, was Taq polymerase first isolated?","e":"Taq DNA polymerase was first found in Thermus aquaticus from hot (volcanic) springs, which is why it is thermally stable.","k":"Taq comes from Thermus aquaticus.","rn":[["","Which organism does Taq come from?","Which organism does Taq come from?","Thermus aquaticus"],["","Where was Thermus aquaticus found?","Where was Thermus aquaticus found?","Hot springs"]]},{"id":40095,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"A suspected RNA virus must be detected by PCR. What extra step is needed?","o":["Reverse transcription of RNA into cDNA (RT-PCR)","Heating the sample in an autoclave to 121 °C for 20 min to release the RNA","Culturing the virus on chocolate agar","Staining the virus with carbolfuchsin","Adding sheep serum to look for a clot"],"e":"For RNA viruses, RT-PCR is used: reverse transcriptase converts viral RNA into complementary DNA (cDNA), which is then amplified with specific ssDNA primers. PCR is also used for other viruses, such as the COVID test.","w":["Autoclaving would destroy the sample.","Viruses do not grow on agar.","Carbolfuchsin is a ZN stain.","Sheep serum is used in the coagulase test."],"k":"RNA genome → RT-PCR via cDNA.","rn":[["","What are the 2 steps of RT-PCR?","What are the 2 steps of RT-PCR?","Convert viral RNA to cDNA; Amplify cDNA with ssDNA primers"]]},{"id":40096,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","a":["rt-pcr","rt pcr","reverse transcription pcr","reverse transcriptase pcr"],"s":"What is the name of the PCR method used for RNA viruses, in which RNA is first converted to cDNA?","e":"RT-PCR (reverse transcription PCR) uses reverse transcriptase to convert viral RNA into cDNA, which is then amplified.","k":"RT-PCR for RNA viruses.","rn":[["","What are the 2 steps of RT-PCR?","What are the 2 steps of RT-PCR?","Convert viral RNA to cDNA; Amplify cDNA with ssDNA primers"],["","What PCR is needed for an RNA virus?","What PCR is needed for an RNA virus?","RT-PCR"]]},{"id":40097,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"How are PCR results usually seen?","o":["A band of the expected size on agarose gel electrophoresis","Golden colonies of the product growing on blood agar after 24 hours","A clot in sheep serum","Yellow colonies on CLED","Green fluorescent cells in culture"],"e":"After thermal cycling (denaturation → annealing → extension, repeated), results are seen by agarose gel electrophoresis as a band of the expected size, stained with ethidium bromide.","w":["Colonies on agar are culture results.","Clotting is the coagulase test.","Yellow CLED shows lactose fermentation.","Green fluorescence is immunofluorescence."],"k":"PCR product: band of expected size on agarose gel.","rn":[["","How are PCR results visualised?","How are PCR results visualised?","Agarose gel electrophoresis"]]},{"id":40098,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"In this PCR figure, which label shows the step at about 50 °C?","o":["2, primer annealing","1, DNA denaturation at about 95 °C","3, primer extension at about 72 °C","4, the agarose gel showing products","The size marker lane M"],"e":"The figure shows 1 DNA denaturation (~95 °C peak); 2 primer annealing (~50 °C); 3 primer extension (~72 °C); 4 an agarose gel showing PCR product bands (lane M = size marker).","w":["Denaturation is the hottest step.","Extension is at about 72 °C.","The gel shows results, not a temperature step.","Lane M is a size marker."],"k":"Annealing is the coolest PCR step.","f":"lm55","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of PCR?","What are the 3 steps of PCR?","Denaturation of dsDNA template; Annealing of primers; Extension of dsDNA molecules; MNEMONIC"],["","What happens during annealing?","What happens during annealing?","Forward and reverse primers; Bind complementary sequences"],["","What is the annealing temperature in PCR?","What is the annealing temperature in PCR?","50 to 70°C"]]},{"id":40099,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"In this extension diagram, what is shown at label 2?","o":["Taq polymerase bound to the primer","The forward primer annealed to the lower strand","Free nucleotides being added","The reverse primer on the upper strand","A melted double strand at 92 °C"],"e":"The extension figure labels 1 the forward primer annealed to the lower strand; 2 Taq polymerase bound to the primer; 3 free nucleotides being added; 4 the reverse primer on the upper strand, also extended by Taq.","w":["The forward primer is label 1.","Free nucleotides are label 3.","The reverse primer is label 4.","Denaturation is a separate figure."],"k":"Extension: Taq adds nucleotides from the primer.","f":"lm53","rn":[["","What happens during extension?","What happens during extension?","DNA polymerase binds primers; Extends from 3' end"],["","Which DNA polymerase is used in PCR?","Which DNA polymerase is used in PCR?","Taq DNA polymerase"]]},{"id":40100,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"A patient with fever, headache, confusion and fits is suspected of viral encephalitis. Which test is commonly used to diagnose it, according to the lecture?","o":["PCR, backed up by brain imaging and spinal fluid cells","CLED agar culture of the spinal fluid, read for lactose fermentation at 24 hours","Ziehl-Neelsen stain of the spinal fluid","The sellotape test","An API strip"],"e":"Viral encephalitis is viral infection of brain tissue with fever, headache, confusion and fits. Causes are many: enteroviruses most commonly (usually mild, severe if the CNS or heart is involved) and herpes viruses (HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV). PCR is commonly used to diagnose it, backed up by brain imaging, cells in spinal fluid and so on.","w":["CLED is for urine bacteria.","ZN is for TB.","The sellotape test is for pinworm.","API strips identify Gram-negative bacteria."],"k":"Viral encephalitis is commonly diagnosed by PCR.","rn":[["","What backs up PCR in viral encephalitis?","What backs up PCR in viral encephalitis?","Brain imaging; Cells in spinal fluid"]]},{"id":40101,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"HIV is mainly diagnosed by serology. Which roles of PCR in HIV are listed?","o":["Confirming early infection, monitoring resistance and monitoring viral load","Growing HIV on blood agar so that the colonies can be counted each week","Staining HIV with auramine","Measuring zones of inhibition around HIV","Replacing all HIV serology"],"e":"HIV is mainly diagnosed by serology. PCR is used to confirm early infection, monitor resistance and monitor treatment response (viral load). More on HIV comes next year.","w":["Viruses do not grow on agar.","Auramine is a TB stain.","Zones of inhibition are for bacterial antibiotic testing.","Serology remains the main diagnostic method."],"k":"HIV PCR: early infection, resistance, viral load.","rn":[["","What are the 3 uses of PCR in HIV?","What are the 3 uses of PCR in HIV?","Confirm early infection; Monitor resistance; Monitor treatment response (viral load)"]]},{"id":40102,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Which statement about the causes of viral encephalitis is consistent with the lecture?","o":["Enteroviruses are most common; herpes viruses (HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV) are also causes","Only bacteria cause encephalitis, so viral tests are never needed","Herpes viruses never cause encephalitis","Encephalitis is caused only by pinworms","Enteroviruses always cause severe disease"],"e":"Viral encephalitis has many causes: enteroviruses are the most common (usually mild, severe if the CNS or heart is involved), and herpes viruses (HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV) are also causes.","w":["The lecture describes viral encephalitis.","Herpes viruses are listed causes.","Pinworms are not a cause.","Enteroviruses are usually mild."],"k":"Encephalitis: enteroviruses (commonest), herpes viruses.","rn":[["","Which 4 herpes viruses cause viral encephalitis?","Which 4 herpes viruses cause viral encephalitis?","HSV; VZV; CMV; EBV"]]},{"id":40103,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"How does whole-genome sequencing (WGS) differ from PCR in what it examines?","o":["WGS sequences the entire DNA; PCR looks for specific unique markers","WGS detects antibodies in serum, whereas PCR detects viral antigens on cells","WGS stains bacteria; PCR cultures them","WGS is used only for parasites","They are identical methods"],"e":"WGS sequences the entire DNA of bacteria and viruses, whereas PCR only looks for specific unique markers. WGS can compare strains (relatedness, evolution) and interrogate for antibiotic resistance genes.","w":["Neither method detects antibodies.","Neither stains or cultures bacteria.","WGS is used for bacteria and viruses.","They differ in scope."],"k":"WGS: whole genome; PCR: specific markers.","rn":[["","PCR vs WGS: what is looked at?","PCR vs WGS: what is looked at?","PCR: unique markers; WGS: entire genome"]]},{"id":40104,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Which uses of WGS are given in the lecture?","o":["Comparing strains and detecting antibiotic resistance genes","Counting white cells in urine to decide whether infection is significant","Diagnosing pinworm","Clotting sheep serum","Lysing red cells for chocolate agar"],"e":"WGS lets you compare strains (how related they are, how they evolve) and interrogate for antibiotic resistance genes. It is set to revolutionise diagnosis (rapid, cheap, desktop platforms) and is integral to surveillance of infectious disease, including foodborne outbreaks.","w":["WBC counts are done by microscopy.","Pinworm is diagnosed by the sellotape test.","Clotting serum is coagulase.","Chocolate agar is made by heating."],"k":"WGS: strain comparison, resistance genes, outbreak surveillance.","rn":[["","What are the 2 uses of whole-genome sequencing?","What are the 2 uses of whole-genome sequencing?","Compare strains (relatedness, evolution); Find antibiotic resistance genes"]]},{"id":40105,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Why is whole human genome sequencing not used routinely in the NHS, according to the lecturer?","o":["It is expensive, even though it can now take about 4 hours","It still takes several years to sequence each human genome","It can only read about 100 base pairs","It cannot be done on human DNA","It needs a live virus"],"e":"The lecturer noted that a whole human genome (about 3.1–3.2 billion base pairs) can now be sequenced in about 4 hours, compared with ~100 base pairs at a time when he started his PhD, but it is expensive, so not used routinely in the NHS.","w":["It now takes about 4 hours.","100 base pairs was the limit years ago.","Human genomes can be sequenced.","No live virus is needed."],"k":"Human WGS: ~4 h but expensive.","rn":[["","How long does sequencing a whole human genome take now?","How long does sequencing a whole human genome take now?","About 4 hours"]]},{"id":40106,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","a":["whole-genome sequencing","whole genome sequencing","wgs"],"s":"Name the technique that sequences the entire DNA of bacteria and viruses and is integral to surveillance of foodborne outbreaks.","e":"Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) sequences entire bacterial and viral DNA to compare strains and find resistance genes, and is integral to infectious disease surveillance.","k":"WGS for surveillance and resistance genes.","rn":[["","What is WGS (whole-genome sequencing) used for in surveillance?","What is WGS (whole-genome sequencing) used for in surveillance?","Surveillance of infectious disease; Including foodborne illness outbreaks"],["","What is whole-genome sequencing?","What is whole-genome sequencing?","Sequencing entire DNA; Of bacteria, viruses"],["","What are the 2 uses of whole-genome sequencing?","What are the 2 uses of whole-genome sequencing?","Compare strains (relatedness, evolution); Find antibiotic resistance genes"]]},{"id":40107,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"Which organisms require tissue culture according to the lecture?","o":["Intracellular organisms: viruses, Mycoplasma and Chlamydia","E. coli and S. aureus","Pinworms","All Gram-positive bacteria, which cannot grow on agar plates","Lactose fermenters"],"e":"Tissue culture is used for intracellular organisms: viruses, Mycoplasma and Chlamydia. It is technically much more challenging, and growth is detected by cytopathic effects or viral protein expression at the cell surface.","w":["These grow on agar.","Pinworms are visualised directly.","Many Gram-positive bacteria grow on agar.","Lactose fermenters grow on CLED."],"k":"Tissue culture: viruses, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia.","rn":[["","What are the 3 intracellular organisms grown in tissue culture?","What are the 3 intracellular organisms grown in tissue culture?","Viruses; Mycoplasma; Chlamydia"]]},{"id":40108,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"What is a cytopathic effect (CPE)?","o":["Virus-induced changes in host cells","Clotting of serum by a bacterial enzyme","A zone of clearing around an antibiotic disc","Yellow colour on CLED agar","A pink stain retained by mycobacteria"],"e":"CPE is structural change in a host cell from viral infection: the virus lyses the cell, or the cell dies without lysis because it cannot reproduce. It shows that the cells are susceptible to the virus, but also why culturing viruses is hard: the virus kills the cells it grows in.","w":["Clotting is coagulase.","Clear zones show antibiotic sensitivity.","Yellow CLED shows lactose fermentation.","Pink retention is acid-fast staining."],"k":"CPE = virus-induced damage to host cells.","rn":[["","What is a cytopathic effect?","What is a cytopathic effect?","Host cell structural changes; From viral infection"],["","What 2 ways does a cytopathic effect occur?","What 2 ways does a cytopathic effect occur?","Virus lyses host cell; Cell dies without lysis; (Can't reproduce)"]]},{"id":40109,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"In this image of a cell line 8 days after rubella infection, what is the cytopathic effect seen in panel B?","o":["Foci of small, circular cells","Golden colonies","Pink acid-fast rods","A clear zone of inhibition around an antibiotic disc on the culture","A band on an agarose gel"],"e":"The rubella figure shows A uninfected, healthy cells and B cells 8 days after infection with wild-type rubella in the SIRC (rabbit cornea) cell line. The CPE is foci of small, circular cells. Rubella (German measles) causes a spotty rash and is serious in pregnancy.","w":["Golden colonies are S. aureus on agar.","Pink rods are acid-fast bacteria.","Clear zones are antibiotic testing.","Gel bands are PCR results."],"k":"Rubella CPE: foci of small, round cells.","f":"lm60"},{"id":40110,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"A monkey kidney cell line infected with influenza is treated with fluorochrome-labelled antibodies against a viral surface protein. What would you see?","o":["Infected cells glowing green","All cells turning yellow","A clot in the tube","Pink-red rods on a blue background","No change in any cell"],"e":"In immunofluorescence, the virus makes a viral-encoded protein expressed on the cell surface (an antigen). Antibodies against it, labelled with a fluorochrome, are added, and infected cells glow green, as in the influenza-infected monkey kidney cell line.","w":["Yellow is a CLED pH change.","Clotting is the coagulase test.","Pink-red rods are ZN-stained mycobacteria.","Infected cells would fluoresce."],"k":"Immunofluorescence: labelled antibodies make infected cells glow.","f":"lm61","rn":[["","Image card: Monkey kidney cell line infected with influenza: what test is this? What is glowing green?","Monkey kidney cell line infected with influenza: what test is this? What is glowing green?","Immunofluorescence; Fluorochrome-labelled antibody on viral protein"]]},{"id":40111,"t":"Tissue Culture","a":["cytopathic effect","cpe","cytopathic"],"s":"What is the term for virus-induced structural changes or death of host cells in tissue culture?","e":"A cytopathic effect (CPE) is structural change in a host cell caused by viral infection; the cell may be lysed or die without lysis.","k":"CPE = cytopathic effect.","rn":[["","What is a cytopathic effect?","What is a cytopathic effect?","Host cell structural changes; From viral infection"]]},{"id":40112,"t":"Tissue Culture","a":["immunofluorescence"],"s":"Name the technique in which fluorochrome-labelled antibodies reveal viral proteins on infected cells.","e":"Immunofluorescence uses antibodies labelled with a fluorochrome against viral proteins on the cell surface, so infected cells glow (green).","k":"Immunofluorescence detects viral antigen on cells.","rn":[["","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","Viral protein; On the cell surface"],["","What is the protein immunofluorescence detects?","What is the protein immunofluorescence detects?","A surface antigen"],["","How are antibodies labelled for immunofluorescence?","How are antibodies labelled for immunofluorescence?","Fluorochrome"]]},{"id":40113,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"A patient has suspected community-acquired pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma. Which test strategy fits the lecture best?","o":["Serology or molecular methods, because Mycoplasma Gram stains poorly and is hard to culture","Gram stain of a sputum sample alone, read in about 20 minutes","CLED agar culture","The sellotape test","Ziehl-Neelsen staining"],"e":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae (~30% of CAP) Gram stains poorly and is difficult to culture. Serology is used for Chlamydia and Mycoplasma, and molecular techniques such as PCR are used for difficult-to-culture organisms.","w":["Sputum Gram stain is unhelpful and misses Mycoplasma.","CLED is for urine.","The sellotape test is for pinworm.","ZN is for TB."],"k":"Mycoplasma: use serology or PCR.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"]]},{"id":40114,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which technique is described as \"most accurate\" for difficult-to-culture organisms?","o":["Molecular techniques (PCR)","Microscopy","Culture on nutrient and selective agar plates in the laboratory","Tissue culture","Urine dip-stick"],"e":"The overview table describes molecular techniques (PCR) as most accurate, microscopy as easy and quick, culture as pretty easy but tricky for some organisms, serology as detecting antibodies, and tissue culture as technically challenging.","w":["Microscopy is easy and quick.","Culture is tricky for some organisms.","Tissue culture is technically challenging.","Dip-sticks are office screening tests."],"k":"PCR = most accurate.","rn":[["","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","What are the 3 techniques for difficult-to-culture organisms?","Serological techniques; Molecular techniques (PCR); Tissue culture"]]},{"id":40115,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"Which pairing of sample and best first lab approach is correct?","o":["CSF with turbidity: Gram stain (and give antibiotics)","Sputum: Gram stain to identify the single pathogen causing the chest infection","Vesicle fluid: ZN stain for herpes","Anal tape: CLED culture","Urine: electron microscopy"],"e":"From a sterile site such as CSF, Gram stain is useful (e.g. Gram-negative diplococci ≈ N. meningitidis), and antibiotics are given straight away. Sputum is non-sterile and Gram stain is often unhelpful; herpes in vesicle fluid is seen by EM; pinworm ova by sellotape microscopy; urine by microscopy and CLED culture.","w":["Sputum is non-sterile and mixed.","Herpes is seen by EM, not ZN.","Pinworm tape is examined by microscopy.","Urine uses light microscopy and culture."],"k":"Sterile-site Gram stain is useful; match the method to the sample.","rn":[["","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Sterile sites; e.g. CSF, joint fluid"]]},{"id":40116,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A newly admitted patient's infection is caused by a highly infectious but not highly virulent organism. Why might the lab still notify public health?","o":["Being highly infectious is enough to pose a public health problem","Only highly virulent organisms are ever notifiable","Notification is only for antibiotic-sensitive organisms","Public health only needs data on viruses","Labs never notify public health"],"e":"Informing the epidemiology spots possible public health problems: an organism need not be highly virulent; being highly infectious is enough. Many diseases are notifiable and must be reported to Public Health England or the equivalent.","w":["High infectiousness alone can matter.","Notification depends on the disease, not sensitivity.","Bacterial and resistance data matter too.","Many diseases are notifiable."],"k":"Highly infectious organisms can be public health problems.","rn":[["","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","How does a lab result inform epidemiology?","Tracks antibiotic resistance; Spots public health threats; (Notifiable diseases)"]]},{"id":40117,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","s":"Which statement about interpreting lab results is consistent with the lecture's aim?","o":["Interpreting the lab data is the clinician's job, not the lab's","The lab always decides the treatment","Clinicians never need to know which bacteria cause which syndromes","Lab results arrive before treatment is needed","Clinicians routinely visit the lab to run tests"],"e":"The lecture aims to give an appreciation of what happens when you send a sample to the lab (a place you are unlikely to visit) and stresses that interpreting lab data is your job as the clinician, not the lab's. Empiric treatment requires knowing which bacteria cause which syndromes and resistance rates.","w":["The clinician interprets and decides.","Knowledge of likely bacteria is needed for empiric treatment.","Results take 2–3 days.","You are unlikely to visit the lab."],"k":"Clinicians interpret lab results."},{"id":40118,"t":"Bacterial Culture","s":"Which technique would identify an unknown Gram-negative isolate by its biochemistry?","o":["An API strip","An electron micrograph","A sellotape test","An IgM serology test","A cytopathic effect assay"],"e":"The API (analytical profile index) test identifies Gram-negative bacteria by up to 20 miniature biochemical tests, matched by software to a database, with about 95% confidence (higher with genetic profiling).","w":["EM is for viruses.","The sellotape test is for pinworm.","Serology detects antibodies.","CPE assays detect viruses in tissue culture."],"k":"API identifies Gram-negatives biochemically.","rn":[["","How many tests are on an API strip?","How many tests are on an API strip?","Up to 20"]]},{"id":40119,"t":"Molecular Techniques: PCR and Sequencing","a":["pcr","polymerase chain reaction"],"s":"Which technique amplifies a small amount of a specific DNA sequence, in theory from one molecule, for testing?","e":"PCR (polymerase chain reaction) takes a small amount of a specific DNA sequence, in theory one molecule, and amplifies it by enzymatic cycling for further testing.","k":"PCR amplifies specific DNA.","rn":[["","What does PCR do?","What does PCR do?","Amplifies a small amount; Of specific DNA"]]},{"id":40120,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"A lab needs to detect a virus that kills its host cells and cannot be seen with a light microscope. Which pair of approaches from the lecture would work?","o":["PCR or serology, or tissue culture with CPE/immunofluorescence","Gram stain and CLED culture","ZN stain and auramine","Disc susceptibility testing","The sellotape test"],"e":"Viruses are difficult to culture because they kill the cells you grow them in, and they cannot be seen by light microscopy (EM is needed). Options are serology (antibodies), PCR/WGS (genes), or tissue culture detecting cytopathic effects or viral proteins by immunofluorescence.","w":["Gram stain and agar culture are for bacteria.","ZN and auramine are for mycobacteria.","Disc testing is for bacterial antibiotic sensitivity.","The sellotape test is for pinworm."],"k":"Viruses: serology, PCR, or tissue culture (CPE, immunofluorescence).","rn":[["","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","Viral protein; On the cell surface"]]},{"id":40121,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A 32-year-old woman has 48 hours of burning pain on passing urine, frequency and urgency. Temperature 39 °C, pulse 90/min, BP 120/80 mmHg, suprapubic tenderness, no loin tenderness, and she is not systemically ill. Dip-stick shows protein, blood, leucocytes and nitrites. She is started on trimethoprim and a sample is sent. On day 3 the lab reports E. coli, trimethoprim resistant, amoxicillin sensitive.<br><br>What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Call her back and switch to amoxicillin","Continue trimethoprim, since empiric choices should never be changed once started","Stop all antibiotics, since E. coli is a commensal that needs no treatment","Repeat the culture and wait another 48 hours before changing anything","Admit her for pyelonephritis because of the nitrites on dip-stick"],"e":"Step 1: the working diagnosis was an uncomplicated UTI (no loin tenderness, not systemically ill), treated empirically with cheap, safe, narrow-spectrum trimethoprim despite about a 30% chance of resistance. Step 2: diagnostic microbiology corrects and focuses treatment: the E. coli is trimethoprim resistant and amoxicillin sensitive, so the GP calls her back and prescribes amoxicillin.","w":["Lab results are used to focus and change empiric treatment.","She has a symptomatic infection needing treatment.","The definitive sensitivity result is already available.","Nitrites indicate infection; there is no loin tenderness or systemic illness to suggest pyelonephritis."],"k":"Empiric treatment first; switch when sensitivities return.","rn":[["","What antibiotic was switched to after the lab result?","What antibiotic was switched to after the lab result?","Amoxicillin (broad spectrum)"]]},{"id":40122,"t":"Microscopy and Gram Staining","s":"A 7-year-old is brought to the emergency department with high fever, headache and photophobia. A lumbar puncture yields cloudy, turbid cerebrospinal fluid. The lab will take 24–48 hours to culture the sample.<br><br>What should be done first, and what is the Gram stain most likely to show?","o":["Give antibiotics immediately; Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils","Wait 48 hours for culture; Gram-positive cocci in clusters","Give antibiotics only if PCR is positive; acid-fast rods","Give no antibiotics, as most meningitis is viral and benign; no organisms will be seen","Repeat the lumbar puncture tomorrow; Gram-negative rods in urine"],"e":"Step 1: fever, headache and photophobia suggest meningitis; turbid CSF indicates potential bacterial infection. Bacterial meningitis can kill within hours, so antibiotics are given straight away while the sample is still sent. Step 2: the Gram stain shows intracellular Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils, suggesting Neisseria meningitidis.","w":["You cannot wait 48 hours with possible bacterial meningitis.","Treatment must not be delayed, and acid-fast rods suggest TB.","Bacterial meningitis must be ruled out quickly, so patients are given antibiotics.","This is a CSF, not urine, sample, and treatment is urgent."],"k":"Turbid CSF: treat immediately; Gram-negative diplococci ≈ N. meningitidis.","rn":[["","Where are the Gram negative diplococci in meningococcal CSF?","Where are the Gram negative diplococci in meningococcal CSF?","Inside neutrophils (intracellular)"],["","Image card: CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","CSF Gram stain from a child with fever and photophobia: what is seen? Likely organism?","Gram negative diplococci in neutrophils; Neisseria meningitidis"]]},{"id":40123,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"An 18-year-old student at the end of freshers' week has fever, sweats, sore throat and swollen neck lymph glands. Serology: EBV IgM negative, EBV IgG negative; CMV IgM negative, CMV IgG positive. One week later: EBV IgM positive, EBV IgG negative; CMV unchanged.<br><br>What is the best interpretation?","o":["Acute EBV infection; immune to CMV from past infection","Acute CMV infection; never infected with EBV","Past EBV infection; acute CMV infection","No infection; the first EBV result proves she is immune","Chronic EBV infection; susceptible to CMV"],"e":"Step 1: IgM marks acute infection and is the first antibody against a new infection; IgG marks a long-standing response. Initially EBV IgM and IgG were negative (susceptible, never infected), and a week later EBV IgM became positive, showing acute EBV infection; antibodies take time to develop. Step 2: CMV IgM negative with IgG positive means CMV immune (infected in the past).","w":["CMV IgM stayed negative, so CMV is not acute.","EBV IgG was negative, and CMV IgM negative rules out acute CMV.","Both EBV antibodies negative means susceptible, not immune.","IgM positive means acute EBV, and CMV IgG positive means immune."],"k":"IgM+ = acute; IgG+/IgM− = past/immune; both − = susceptible.","rn":[["","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","IgM negative, IgG positive means what?","Immune (past infection)"],["","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Repeat test one week later: EBV IgM positive, IgG negative. Diagnosis?","Acute EBV infection"],["","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM vs IgG: what does each show?","IgM: acute infection; IgG: long-standing immune response"]]},{"id":40124,"t":"Difficult-to-Culture Organisms and Serology","s":"A 70-year-old with community-acquired pneumonia has fever, cough, chest pain and confusion. Sputum Gram stain shows a mixture of organisms, and culture grows nothing significant after 48 hours. He is not improving.<br><br>Which is the best explanation and next approach?","o":["An organism such as Mycoplasma or Chlamydia that Grams poorly; use serology or PCR","Streptococcus pneumoniae, which never grows in culture; repeat the sputum Gram stain daily","Haemophilus influenzae, which is acid-fast; use a Ziehl-Neelsen stain","No infection, because culture was negative; stop investigating","A virus seen on Gram stain; use an API strip to identify it"],"e":"Step 1: in CAP, S. pneumoniae (~30%) and H. influenzae (~10%) Gram stain and culture easily, but Mycoplasma pneumoniae (~30%) and Chlamydia pneumoniae (~10%) Gram poorly and are difficult to culture, so about 40% could be missed. Sputum is also a non-sterile site, so its Gram stain is often unhelpful. Step 2: difficult-to-culture organisms need serology (also used for Chlamydia and Mycoplasma) or molecular techniques.","w":["S. pneumoniae Grams and cultures easily.","H. influenzae Grams and cultures easily and is not described as acid-fast.","Culture misses difficult-to-culture organisms.","Viruses are not seen on Gram stain, and API identifies Gram-negative bacteria."],"k":"CAP with negative culture: think Mycoplasma/Chlamydia → serology/PCR.","rn":[["","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Which 2 CAP organisms Gram poorly and are hard to culture?","Mycoplasma pneumoniae; Chlamydia pneumoniae"]]},{"id":40125,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"A 45-year-old with weight loss and a chronic cough has sputum sent for microscopy. The Gram stain shows very little. A second stain uses carbolfuchsin, heat fixing, acid-alcohol and methylene blue, and shows pink-red rods on a blue background.<br><br>What explains these findings?","o":["Mycobacteria are acid-fast: waxy mycolic acid traps the stain","The rods are Gram negative, so they lose the purple stain and take up the pink","The rods lack a cell wall, so no stain can enter","The rods are viruses visible only by electron microscopy","The rods are fungi stained by silver"],"e":"Step 1: TB (mycobacteria) does not stain well with Gram stain, so the Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) stain is used: carbolfuchsin, heat fix, acid-alcohol decolourisation, methylene blue counterstain. Step 2: mycobacteria are acid-fast because waxy mycolic acid in their wall stops the stain leaving, so they stay pink while non-acid-fast organisms turn blue. Auramine is a more sensitive and rapid fluorescent alternative.","w":["Gram-negative bacteria stain pink on Gram stain; they are not acid-fast.","Mycobacteria have a thick mycolic acid wall.","These are bacteria seen on light microscopy.","Silver (GMS) stain is used for Pneumocystis."],"k":"ZN: acid-fast mycobacteria stay pink (mycolic acid).","rn":[["","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Why are mycobacteria acid-fast?","Waxy mycolic acid wall; Retains the stain"]]},{"id":40126,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A urine sample is plated on CLED agar. After 24 hours, colonies and the surrounding medium have turned yellow. A second sample from another patient grows blue colonies on CLED.<br><br>What do these colours indicate?","o":["Yellow: lactose fermenter (typical of E. coli); blue: non-lactose fermenter","Yellow: haemolytic organism that lyses red cells; blue: non-haemolytic organism","Yellow: Gram positive; blue: Gram negative","Yellow: antibiotic resistant; blue: antibiotic sensitive","Yellow: acid-fast organism; blue: non-acid-fast organism"],"e":"Step 1: CLED (cystine lactose electrolyte deficient) agar is used for bacteria from urine. A bacterium that ferments lactose makes acid, and the pH indicator changes colour. Step 2: lactose fermenters give yellow colonies (typical of E. coli); non-lactose fermenters give blue colonies.","w":["Haemolysis is shown on blood agar.","Gram status is shown by Gram staining.","Resistance is shown by disc testing.","Acid-fastness is shown by ZN staining."],"k":"CLED: yellow = lactose fermenter (E. coli).","rn":[["","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Lactose fermenters vs non-fermenters on CLED: colony colour?","Fermenters: yellow; Non-fermenters: blue"]]},{"id":40127,"t":"Other Stains and Direct Visualisation","s":"Two samples arrive in the lab. Sample 1 is joint fluid from a hot, swollen knee. Sample 2 is sputum from a patient with a chest infection. Both are Gram stained. Sample 1 shows a single type of organism; sample 2 shows Gram-negative cocci, Gram-positive cocci in clusters and chains, and Gram-positive rods.<br><br>Which sample's Gram stain is more useful, and why?","o":["Sample 1, because joint fluid is a sterile site where one pathogen is expected","Sample 2, because seeing more types of organism makes the diagnosis more certain","Both equally, because Gram stain is always definitive","Neither, because Gram stain cannot be used on body fluids","Sample 2, because sputum is a sterile site"],"e":"Step 1: joint fluid is a sterile site, where bacteria are not normally found, so a Gram stain showing one organism is useful. Step 2: sputum is a non-sterile site loaded with bacteria, debris and cells, so in many cases its Gram stain is not useful; the mixture of organisms makes it hard to interpret.","w":["A mixture of organisms makes interpretation harder.","Gram stain is most useful from sterile sites only.","Gram stains of CSF, urine and joint fluid are used.","Sputum is a non-sterile site."],"k":"Gram stain is most useful from sterile sites.","rn":[["","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","When is Gram staining for bacteria most useful?","Sample from sterile site; Single pathogen expected"],["","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Where are microscopy and Gram stains useful from?","Sterile sites; e.g. CSF, joint fluid"]]},{"id":40128,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A GP sends a urine sample to the lab on Monday morning, not marked urgent. The patient asks when she will know which antibiotic definitely works.<br><br>What is the most realistic answer, and why?","o":["About 2–3 days: 24 h for growth, up to 48 h for sensitivities","About 10 minutes, as urine microscopy is instant","About 4 hours, as the whole genome will be sequenced","About 2 weeks, as all bacteria must first be grown in tissue culture cell lines","Immediately, as the dip-stick shows the sensitivity"],"e":"Step 1: the sample may not be processed until later in the day unless marked urgent; culture takes 24 hours for first information and up to 48 hours for antibiotic resistance (definitive information). Step 2: realistically it takes 2–3 days, so the patient must be managed (empirically) in the meantime.","w":["Microscopy is quick but does not give sensitivities.","Whole-genome sequencing is not used routinely in the NHS.","Tissue culture is for intracellular organisms.","A dip-stick does not test antibiotic sensitivity."],"k":"Culture: 24 h any result, 48 h definitive; 2–3 days in practice.","rn":[["","Realistically, how long until a GP gets a culture and sensitivity result?","Realistically, how long until a GP gets a culture and sensitivity result?","About 2 to 3 days"]]},{"id":40129,"t":"Urine Microscopy and Culture","s":"A lab spreads a high density of a patient's bacteria across an agar plate and adds paper discs, each soaked in a different antibiotic. After incubation, disc A has a wide clear zone around it, while disc B has bacteria growing right up to its edge.<br><br>How should these results be read?","o":["Sensitive to A (zone of inhibition); resistant to B (no zone)","Resistant to A (zone of inhibition); sensitive to B (no zone)","Sensitive to both, as all discs contain antibiotic","The organism is a lactose fermenter","The organism is Gram positive because of the zone"],"e":"Step 1: the antibiotic diffuses from each disc into the agar, and a clear zone of inhibition appears where it stops growth. Step 2: the zone diameter is compared with disk-diffusion standards: a clear zone means sensitive, while no zone (growth up to the disc) means resistant.","w":["A zone means the antibiotic stopped growth.","Growth up to disc B means resistance.","Lactose fermentation is read on CLED.","Gram status is not read from zones."],"k":"Zone of inhibition = sensitive; no zone = resistant.","rn":[["","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone vs growth up to the disc: what does each mean?","Clear zone: sensitive; Growth up to disc: resistant"]]},{"id":40130,"t":"Tissue Culture","s":"A virus is suspected in a patient's sample. The lab adds the sample to a cell line. After 8 days, the cells form foci of small, circular cells compared with uninfected controls. In a second test, fluorochrome-labelled antibodies against a viral surface protein are added to infected cells.<br><br>What do the two methods detect?","o":["Cytopathic effect; viral protein on the cell surface by immunofluorescence","Lactose fermentation; acid-fast staining of the virus","Zones of inhibition; Gram staining of viral particles","IgM in serum; IgG in serum","Haemolysis on blood agar; API metabolic profile"],"e":"Step 1: tissue culture is used for intracellular organisms (viruses, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia); a cytopathic effect (CPE) is a virus-induced structural change in host cells, e.g. foci of small circular cells with rubella. Step 2: immunofluorescence detects a viral-encoded protein expressed on the cell surface using fluorochrome-labelled antibodies, so infected cells glow.","w":["These are bacterial culture and staining methods.","These are antibiotic and bacterial staining methods.","These are serology findings in the patient's blood.","These are bacterial culture and identification methods."],"k":"Tissue culture: CPE or immunofluorescence.","rn":[["","What are the 2 main ways to detect organisms in tissue culture?","What are the 2 main ways to detect organisms in tissue culture?","Cytopathic effect on cells; Viral proteins at cell surface"],["","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","What does immunofluorescence detect in tissue culture?","Viral protein; On the cell surface"]]}]);
